1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,425

2
00:00:05,425 --> 00:00:07,800
Let's recap what we've talked
about in the last couple of

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:10,040
lectures to lead into today.

4
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:13,480
So for policy
gradients, we started

5
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:15,740
with our first reinforcement
learning algorithm,

6
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:17,360
where our goal was
to run a policy

7
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:21,440
to collect a batch of data,
and improve the policy using

8
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:24,200
that batch of data, and
then repeat this process so

9
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:26,280
that your approach
can get better

10
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:30,000
and better with experience.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,680
And the way that we actually
improve the policy using

12
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:36,120
this data is this
equation that we've

13
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:40,320
looked at where we collect
samples from the policy.

14
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:44,440
We then try to increase
the probability for actions

15
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:48,680
in the data that have a
high reward in the future.

16
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:51,280
And then we found that if we
subtracted a baseline that's

17
00:00:51,280 --> 00:00:55,240
the average reward, that
encourages us to have the policy

18
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:57,880
do more of the above average
things and less of the

19
00:00:57,880 --> 00:00:58,910
below average things.

20
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:02,400

21
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:04,660
Right now everything
here requires

22
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:07,797
that the data be sampled
from the exact policy

23
00:01:07,797 --> 00:01:09,880
that you're going to be
taking a gradient step on.

24
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:12,540
And we talked about how we can
use importance weights this term

25
00:01:12,540 --> 00:01:18,700
right here in order to do
multiple gradient steps so

26
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:21,140
that we don't have to be
collect a new batch of data

27
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:25,540
every single time we want
to take one gradient step.

28
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:27,900
We also talked about
value functions.

29
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:32,900
And we talked about how a
value for a policy of a state

30
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:36,860
is the future expected
rewards starting at state S

31
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:39,540
and following pi thereafter.

32
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:41,520
And we talked
about a Q function,

33
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:44,100
which is very similar
to a value function,

34
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:45,760
except instead of
starting at state s,

35
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:48,140
you're going to start at state
s and then take action a,

36
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:52,660
and then follow the policy.

37
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:56,280
And we can think about
these as follows.

38
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:58,090
So if we have a
given state that we

39
00:01:58,090 --> 00:02:01,170
want to estimate the
value of for a policy,

40
00:02:01,170 --> 00:02:02,810
then we can run that policy.

41
00:02:02,810 --> 00:02:06,670
And the value is capturing
the expected sum of rewards,

42
00:02:06,670 --> 00:02:09,370
so basically the sum of rewards
of each of these trajectories

43
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:14,810
averaged over these trajectories
for that state and that policy.

44
00:02:14,810 --> 00:02:20,330
And we can write this as the
summation over future time

45
00:02:20,330 --> 00:02:25,330
steps of our reward if
we're starting at st

46
00:02:25,330 --> 00:02:28,730
and following our policy pi.

47
00:02:28,730 --> 00:02:32,450
And then for the Q function,
instead of starting at s

48
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:34,350
and running the policy,
we'll start at s,

49
00:02:34,350 --> 00:02:37,170
take a, and then run the policy.

50
00:02:37,170 --> 00:02:42,890
And so we will then be
averaging the sum of rewards

51
00:02:42,890 --> 00:02:46,970
over the trajectories that
were a result of running

52
00:02:46,970 --> 00:02:51,027
the policy after taking
action a at state s.

53
00:02:51,027 --> 00:02:52,110
We can write this as this.

54
00:02:52,110 --> 00:02:55,650
Again, the sum over the
future of the future rewards

55
00:02:55,650 --> 00:02:58,510
conditioning on starting
in state s taking action a,

56
00:02:58,510 --> 00:03:01,320
and then running the
policy in the future.

57
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:03,970

58
00:03:03,970 --> 00:03:04,470
Great.

59
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:06,970
Then we also talked about
the advantage function.

60
00:03:06,970 --> 00:03:08,470
And this is,
essentially how much

61
00:03:08,470 --> 00:03:11,950
better is it to take one action
than to follow the policy.

62
00:03:11,950 --> 00:03:14,390
And we can think of
this as the Q function

63
00:03:14,390 --> 00:03:16,550
for that state and
action minus the value

64
00:03:16,550 --> 00:03:18,450
function at that state.

65
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:23,870
And these are all for
a particular policy pi.

66
00:03:23,870 --> 00:03:26,350
Now to think a little bit
more about intuitively

67
00:03:26,350 --> 00:03:28,950
what value functions
and Q functions mean,

68
00:03:28,950 --> 00:03:32,670
we can take a look at
just one quick example.

69
00:03:32,670 --> 00:03:35,030
So I think I
mentioned in lecture 1

70
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:39,790
this example of how DeepMind
developed an AI system that

71
00:03:39,790 --> 00:03:45,750
could outperform and
beat the champion at Go,

72
00:03:45,750 --> 00:03:47,990
and this system
was called AlphaGo.

73
00:03:47,990 --> 00:03:50,350
And they used reinforcement
learning to do this.

74
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:53,290
And if you were to think
about this example,

75
00:03:53,290 --> 00:03:56,450
say the reward is the
outcome of the game,

76
00:03:56,450 --> 00:04:00,010
whether or not
AlphaGo won or lost,

77
00:04:00,010 --> 00:04:02,330
and then the value
function you can

78
00:04:02,330 --> 00:04:05,090
think of as the
expected outcome given

79
00:04:05,090 --> 00:04:09,410
the state of the board and
the policy that's being run.

80
00:04:09,410 --> 00:04:11,650
And so if you have
a better policy,

81
00:04:11,650 --> 00:04:13,970
the value function is
going to be higher.

82
00:04:13,970 --> 00:04:15,970
And likewise, if
you're in a board state

83
00:04:15,970 --> 00:04:18,550
where things are looking
good for that policy,

84
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:21,170
then you're also going to
have a higher value function.

85
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:23,890
And so the value function is
influenced both by the policy

86
00:04:23,890 --> 00:04:26,010
and by the state.

87
00:04:26,010 --> 00:04:29,650
And yeah, so that's
an example of what

88
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:30,950
a value function looks like.

89
00:04:30,950 --> 00:04:33,670
And likewise, a Q
function would be similar,

90
00:04:33,670 --> 00:04:36,030
except instead of running
the policy from the state,

91
00:04:36,030 --> 00:04:38,530
you'd be considering a
particular action first

92
00:04:38,530 --> 00:04:42,170
before running the policy.

93
00:04:42,170 --> 00:04:46,430
So we talked about actor-critic
methods on Friday last week.

94
00:04:46,430 --> 00:04:51,930
And we introduced
this equation, which

95
00:04:51,930 --> 00:04:54,630
looks a lot like
policy gradients

96
00:04:54,630 --> 00:04:56,510
where we're summing
over examples.

97
00:04:56,510 --> 00:05:00,510
Except now, instead of using
the sum of rewards in a given

98
00:05:00,510 --> 00:05:03,910
trajectory, we're using
our estimate of how good

99
00:05:03,910 --> 00:05:06,030
an action is or the advantage.

100
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:10,270
And this comes from the sum of
rewards which is the Q function,

101
00:05:10,270 --> 00:05:13,377
minus the average sum of
rewards which is the value.

102
00:05:13,377 --> 00:05:15,710
And you can think of this as
being different from policy

103
00:05:15,710 --> 00:05:17,793
gradients, and that we're
estimating which actions

104
00:05:17,793 --> 00:05:19,950
are good and bad, and then
encouraging the policy

105
00:05:19,950 --> 00:05:22,300
to do more of the stuff
that we think is good.

106
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:25,630

107
00:05:25,630 --> 00:05:28,330
And then we talked about
how to actually estimate

108
00:05:28,330 --> 00:05:29,870
what's good and bad.

109
00:05:29,870 --> 00:05:31,850
This is referred to
as policy evaluation.

110
00:05:31,850 --> 00:05:34,230
And we can fit a value
function, a neural network

111
00:05:34,230 --> 00:05:37,750
that takes as input the state
and outputs a scalar value.

112
00:05:37,750 --> 00:05:43,536
And we fit this as
trying to predict

113
00:05:43,536 --> 00:05:47,310
either just the sum of
rewards or the next reward

114
00:05:47,310 --> 00:05:49,950
plus the value function
where we're bootstrapping off

115
00:05:49,950 --> 00:05:54,580
of our own estimates, or the sum
of a short trajectory of rewards

116
00:05:54,580 --> 00:05:58,220
plus the value
function after that.

117
00:05:58,220 --> 00:05:59,940
And then this
advantage function is

118
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:01,940
equal to the reward
plus the next value

119
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:04,130
function minus the
current value function.

120
00:06:04,130 --> 00:06:06,760

121
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:09,560
So let's get into the
main topic of today,

122
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:12,140
which is that we talked
about actor-critic methods.

123
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:13,940
Last week we were
primarily considering

124
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:15,900
on policy actor-critic methods.

125
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:20,620
And today we're going to
get into two real algorithms

126
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:23,660
that people use, PPO and SAC.

127
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:26,700
And we will start from the
actor-critic formulation

128
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:31,500
and see how we can make them
off-policy to be more efficient.

129
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:36,460
And at the end, we'll talk about
how PPO, SAC, and imitation

130
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:40,042
learning compare to each other.

131
00:06:40,042 --> 00:06:41,500
And yeah, so the
key learning goals

132
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:44,580
is really the key concepts
behind practical algorithms

133
00:06:44,580 --> 00:06:45,690
like PPO and SAC.

134
00:06:45,690 --> 00:06:48,300

135
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:51,760
So we have our
actor-critic method.

136
00:06:51,760 --> 00:06:54,040
These are the steps
that we go through

137
00:06:54,040 --> 00:06:57,600
for an on-policy actor-critic
method where we sample data,

138
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:00,040
fit rewards with our
value function, then

139
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:02,720
we estimate the advantage with
this equation here for all

140
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:08,680
of our states and actions
in this set of data,

141
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:11,720
then we estimate what the
gradient of our policy is

142
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:14,720
and update our gradient.

143
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:17,300
Now, if we want to be able to
take multiple gradient steps,

144
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:21,160
we need to be able to
evaluate our gradient not just

145
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:24,960
at the current policy
that collected the data--

146
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:31,242
but, for example,
after we take step 5,

147
00:07:31,242 --> 00:07:33,200
we're now going to have
a new set of parameters

148
00:07:33,200 --> 00:07:36,760
that were different from what
was collected the data in step

149
00:07:36,760 --> 00:07:38,360
1.

150
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:40,480
And so what we're
going to do is we're

151
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:43,240
going to use importance weights,
which we saw in the policy

152
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:44,440
gradient lecture.

153
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:47,520
And so our new
objective is going to--

154
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:52,780
or our new gradient is going
to look a little bit different.

155
00:07:52,780 --> 00:07:56,340
So say that we collected
data with pi theta.

156
00:07:56,340 --> 00:07:59,965
Now we've taken a gradient step,
and we have pi theta prime.

157
00:07:59,965 --> 00:08:01,340
So we want to be
able to evaluate

158
00:08:01,340 --> 00:08:06,420
the gradient of the
objective at theta prime.

159
00:08:06,420 --> 00:08:10,260
And to do this, we
will reuse the data

160
00:08:10,260 --> 00:08:14,500
that we collected from
pi theta in step 1.

161
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:19,480
And we're then going to use the
same gradient step as before,

162
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:24,820
but we're going to weight the
gradient term with an importance

163
00:08:24,820 --> 00:08:30,340
ratio that is a ratio
between our new policy

164
00:08:30,340 --> 00:08:33,580
pi theta prime, and the policy
that was used to collect

165
00:08:33,580 --> 00:08:35,929
the data which is pi theta.

166
00:08:35,929 --> 00:08:41,860

167
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:44,203
So this is our importance
weight term right here.

168
00:08:44,203 --> 00:08:45,620
And the rest will
look the same as

169
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:56,600
before where we'll be
evaluating the gradient of log

170
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:01,580
pi times the advantage
or advantage estimate.

171
00:09:01,580 --> 00:09:05,160
This advantage estimate
will still be for the policy

172
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:07,720
before taking a
gradient step pi theta,

173
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:17,840
and this will be for
the s, a pair here.

174
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:21,120
So this is our new gradient.

175
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:22,720
So that's here.

176
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:26,320
Now, in principle,
once we've done this,

177
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:29,840
this means that we can take
multiple gradient steps.

178
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:34,680
So we can sample a batch
of data once and then

179
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:40,720
fit our advantage estimates,
and then compute our gradient,

180
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:43,082
take a gradient step, and
then recompute our gradient,

181
00:09:43,082 --> 00:09:44,540
take a gradient
step, and so forth.

182
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:46,440
And the only thing
that's going to change

183
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:49,870
as we take more gradient steps
is this importance weight here.

184
00:09:49,870 --> 00:09:51,590
And of course
we'll be doing back

185
00:09:51,590 --> 00:09:53,630
propagation on
our new parameters

186
00:09:53,630 --> 00:09:56,198
rather than our old parameters.

187
00:09:56,198 --> 00:09:57,990
And then our advantage
estimates will still

188
00:09:57,990 --> 00:10:00,950
be based off of the old policy.

189
00:10:00,950 --> 00:10:03,920
Now, there's something
that can go wrong here.

190
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:06,670

191
00:10:06,670 --> 00:10:09,443
And to see what
can go wrong, it's

192
00:10:09,443 --> 00:10:11,610
helpful to actually look
at the surrogate objective.

193
00:10:11,610 --> 00:10:13,310
Right now we're
looking at the gradient

194
00:10:13,310 --> 00:10:15,430
that we're going to be applying.

195
00:10:15,430 --> 00:10:17,510
But when we actually
implement this in practice,

196
00:10:17,510 --> 00:10:20,176
we'll be having some
surrogate objective

197
00:10:20,176 --> 00:10:23,110
that we construct
in order to use

198
00:10:23,110 --> 00:10:27,750
auto differentiation to get the
gradients for free from PyTorch.

199
00:10:27,750 --> 00:10:30,670
And so if we were to write
down the surrogate objective

200
00:10:30,670 --> 00:10:33,390
for this, we need to
formulate some objectives such

201
00:10:33,390 --> 00:10:38,910
that the gradient of
our surrogate objective

202
00:10:38,910 --> 00:10:44,150
is equal to this right here.

203
00:10:44,150 --> 00:10:49,370
Now, to do that, we
can remember that we

204
00:10:49,370 --> 00:10:53,850
have this convenient
identity where

205
00:10:53,850 --> 00:10:56,570
probability times the
grad log of a probability

206
00:10:56,570 --> 00:11:00,270
is equal to the gradient of
the original probability.

207
00:11:00,270 --> 00:11:04,610
This is the same log trick
that we've been seeing.

208
00:11:04,610 --> 00:11:06,730
We used to formulate the
original policy gradient

209
00:11:06,730 --> 00:11:08,353
objective.

210
00:11:08,353 --> 00:11:09,770
And the way this
comes in handy is

211
00:11:09,770 --> 00:11:14,370
that we actually see this
pi grad log pi right here.

212
00:11:14,370 --> 00:11:20,170
And so this means that this
is equal to the gradient of pi

213
00:11:20,170 --> 00:11:21,770
theta prime.

214
00:11:21,770 --> 00:11:25,330
And if we want to then construct
a surrogate objective such

215
00:11:25,330 --> 00:11:31,210
that its gradient is this,
then the surrogate objective

216
00:11:31,210 --> 00:11:35,010
is simply going to be--

217
00:11:35,010 --> 00:11:40,570
we can replace this with
pi theta of a given s.

218
00:11:40,570 --> 00:11:45,350
And then we'll still have
the other terms pi theta,

219
00:11:45,350 --> 00:11:49,770
and the advantage right here.

220
00:11:49,770 --> 00:11:55,050

221
00:11:55,050 --> 00:11:57,110
Well, let's just say
this is just over i,

222
00:11:57,110 --> 00:12:01,950
all the examples
are in our batch.

223
00:12:01,950 --> 00:12:04,510
And so the way that
I formulated this

224
00:12:04,510 --> 00:12:07,030
is the gradient of this
surrogate objective

225
00:12:07,030 --> 00:12:09,910
is equal to this right here.

226
00:12:09,910 --> 00:12:12,630
And we see that if we take the
gradient of this with respect

227
00:12:12,630 --> 00:12:16,230
to theta prime, we get grad
theta prime right here.

228
00:12:16,230 --> 00:12:19,230
And the gradient of
theta prime of pi

229
00:12:19,230 --> 00:12:26,190
is equal to pi theta prime
times grad log pi theta prime.

230
00:12:26,190 --> 00:12:28,430
So you can see
this on the slides.

231
00:12:28,430 --> 00:12:30,530
So here's our surrogate
objective right here.

232
00:12:30,530 --> 00:12:33,070

233
00:12:33,070 --> 00:12:35,452
Now, if we look at the
surrogate objective--

234
00:12:35,452 --> 00:12:36,910
so the policy is
going to be trying

235
00:12:36,910 --> 00:12:42,930
to maximize this when we take
gradient steps on our policy.

236
00:12:42,930 --> 00:12:49,370
And if we're trying to
maximize this objective,

237
00:12:49,370 --> 00:12:50,750
we have our advantage estimates.

238
00:12:50,750 --> 00:12:52,490
And so the thing that the
policy will want to do

239
00:12:52,490 --> 00:12:55,210
is it will probably want to take
the advantage estimates that

240
00:12:55,210 --> 00:12:59,690
are positive and increase the
probability of our actions

241
00:12:59,690 --> 00:13:02,850
for those, especially if
there are actions that

242
00:13:02,850 --> 00:13:05,810
had very low probability
that we want to increase this

243
00:13:05,810 --> 00:13:09,730
a lot so that we can get a
very high ratio here, such

244
00:13:09,730 --> 00:13:12,410
that we can increase the
likelihood of actions

245
00:13:12,410 --> 00:13:13,510
for high advantages.

246
00:13:13,510 --> 00:13:15,030
And then for
negative advantages,

247
00:13:15,030 --> 00:13:18,250
we'll want to decrease
the probability.

248
00:13:18,250 --> 00:13:20,630
Now, this all seems
reasonable here.

249
00:13:20,630 --> 00:13:22,130
The thing that's a
little bit tricky

250
00:13:22,130 --> 00:13:24,010
is that these
advantages are still

251
00:13:24,010 --> 00:13:26,050
based off of the old policy.

252
00:13:26,050 --> 00:13:31,570
And they were estimated based
on a single batch of data.

253
00:13:31,570 --> 00:13:34,130
And so if you take too
many gradient steps

254
00:13:34,130 --> 00:13:38,770
on this surrogate objective,
it means you might actually,

255
00:13:38,770 --> 00:13:40,430
in some ways, overfit.

256
00:13:40,430 --> 00:13:43,160
You might be using
these advantages

257
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:45,200
at some point when
they become out of date

258
00:13:45,200 --> 00:13:49,600
and may no longer be
applicable to your new policy.

259
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:52,280
And so basically the
challenge that can come up

260
00:13:52,280 --> 00:13:54,370
is you might get really
unstable learning.

261
00:13:54,370 --> 00:13:58,080

262
00:13:58,080 --> 00:14:00,160
As a rough visual
for what this might

263
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:06,280
look like is if you
were to plot the--

264
00:14:06,280 --> 00:14:13,160
let's say that this is
the number of examples

265
00:14:13,160 --> 00:14:16,680
of state actions that
you've collected,

266
00:14:16,680 --> 00:14:21,080
and then the y-axis
is the rewards

267
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:24,580
that your policy is seeing
over the course of learning

268
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:27,280
as it collects more data.

269
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:31,880
If you take only one gradient
step for every batch of data

270
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:38,312
that you collect,
then your learning

271
00:14:38,312 --> 00:14:40,740
might look something
like this, where

272
00:14:40,740 --> 00:14:43,300
it learns very slowly because
you can only take one gradient

273
00:14:43,300 --> 00:14:44,980
step for each batch
of data, and that

274
00:14:44,980 --> 00:14:48,860
means that it's going to take
many, many data points in order

275
00:14:48,860 --> 00:14:50,500
to learn.

276
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:53,370
So this is for
one gradient step.

277
00:14:53,370 --> 00:14:59,020

278
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:01,520
Now, say, you maybe
increase this a little bit,

279
00:15:01,520 --> 00:15:05,060
so you take five
gradient steps, then

280
00:15:05,060 --> 00:15:06,680
you should learn a
little bit faster.

281
00:15:06,680 --> 00:15:10,380
So maybe you learn
something like this

282
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:13,560
because you're taking more
gradient steps per data point.

283
00:15:13,560 --> 00:15:16,320
So with respect to the amount
of data that you're collecting,

284
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:20,420
your reward should
increase more.

285
00:15:20,420 --> 00:15:22,780
But then if you try to
get a little bit greedy

286
00:15:22,780 --> 00:15:25,920
and do a lot more gradient
steps on this objective,

287
00:15:25,920 --> 00:15:27,740
you might start to
overfit your advantages.

288
00:15:27,740 --> 00:15:30,140
And you'll get something
where maybe you'll actually

289
00:15:30,140 --> 00:15:33,260
start to increase
reward very quickly,

290
00:15:33,260 --> 00:15:36,060
but then learning might be
unstable, and at some point

291
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:40,520
it might even collapse
because your policy will

292
00:15:40,520 --> 00:15:41,580
overfit those advantages.

293
00:15:41,580 --> 00:15:43,540
You'll get some
really bad policy,

294
00:15:43,540 --> 00:15:46,800
or a policy that is increased
the probability to 1

295
00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:50,160
for some advantage but done so
in a way that makes the policy

296
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:51,840
performance poor.

297
00:15:51,840 --> 00:15:54,082
And so this would be--

298
00:15:54,082 --> 00:15:56,540
I don't know exactly how many
gradient steps this would be,

299
00:15:56,540 --> 00:16:01,940
but this would be more
gradient steps than the others.

300
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:04,400
And this would be to many
gradient steps essentially.

301
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:07,013
So the question is, what is the
behavior of an overfit policy

302
00:16:07,013 --> 00:16:07,930
look like in practice?

303
00:16:07,930 --> 00:16:13,440

304
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:17,040
It really depends
on the problem.

305
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:20,020
As one example, if it's
overfitting quite a bit,

306
00:16:20,020 --> 00:16:23,520
it might try to be assigning
a very, very high probability

307
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:26,200
to one particular
action that has

308
00:16:26,200 --> 00:16:28,040
a high advantage in the data.

309
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:30,360
And when it does that,
it might actually also

310
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:34,040
increase the probability
of other actions

311
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:36,910
around it just because neural
networks are somewhat smooth.

312
00:16:36,910 --> 00:16:42,030
And when you do that, it might
be a very deterministic policy

313
00:16:42,030 --> 00:16:43,040
that no longer explores.

314
00:16:43,040 --> 00:16:52,070

315
00:16:52,070 --> 00:16:53,870
I don't know exactly
what would happen,

316
00:16:53,870 --> 00:16:59,790
but if you had a 2D agent
that's moving around in a grid,

317
00:16:59,790 --> 00:17:02,110
and maybe your goal
is to get here,

318
00:17:02,110 --> 00:17:06,069
then a good policy
would go here.

319
00:17:06,069 --> 00:17:10,310
And a policy that's overfit, I
would expect to potentially--

320
00:17:10,310 --> 00:17:12,950
maybe, for example, it
learns that going right

321
00:17:12,950 --> 00:17:16,470
is good but doesn't figure out
that you need to stop here.

322
00:17:16,470 --> 00:17:18,490
And does it figure out
that you need to go up?

323
00:17:18,490 --> 00:17:20,670
Then what the overfit
policy might do

324
00:17:20,670 --> 00:17:23,069
is it might take a
straight line here and then

325
00:17:23,069 --> 00:17:26,349
keep on trying to go
roughly in this direction

326
00:17:26,349 --> 00:17:28,910
and then end up in the
corner because it's

327
00:17:28,910 --> 00:17:32,810
overfit to one batch of data
that told it to go this way

328
00:17:32,810 --> 00:17:35,530
but didn't figure out
that it needs to stop.

329
00:17:35,530 --> 00:17:36,350
Yeah, exactly.

330
00:17:36,350 --> 00:17:40,330
So this is 5 gradient
steps on five loops of this

331
00:17:40,330 --> 00:17:43,610
before you then collect
a new batch of data

332
00:17:43,610 --> 00:17:47,530
and then refit your
value function.

333
00:17:47,530 --> 00:17:53,210
Currently it's refit only on
the current batch of data.

334
00:17:53,210 --> 00:17:55,850
Later this lecture, we'll
see how we can potentially

335
00:17:55,850 --> 00:17:57,580
reuse previous batches.

336
00:17:57,580 --> 00:17:59,330
So the question is,
what is the importance

337
00:17:59,330 --> 00:18:03,490
of using the surrogate objective
versus the original formulation?

338
00:18:03,490 --> 00:18:05,410
This is the same formulation.

339
00:18:05,410 --> 00:18:07,652

340
00:18:07,652 --> 00:18:09,610
I think it's a little
bit more intuitive if you

341
00:18:09,610 --> 00:18:11,570
look at the surrogate
objective to understand

342
00:18:11,570 --> 00:18:14,970
that these importance weights
are really trying to make it--

343
00:18:14,970 --> 00:18:16,865
it's very easy to
increase the probability.

344
00:18:16,865 --> 00:18:18,490
You can also see that
with the gradient

345
00:18:18,490 --> 00:18:20,073
as well, where it's
going to be trying

346
00:18:20,073 --> 00:18:21,862
to increase the probability.

347
00:18:21,862 --> 00:18:23,570
But I think it's just
a little bit easier

348
00:18:23,570 --> 00:18:25,130
to see with the
surrogate objective.

349
00:18:25,130 --> 00:18:26,748
We also, in a minute,
see how we can

350
00:18:26,748 --> 00:18:29,290
change the surrogate objective,
and it gets a little bit more

351
00:18:29,290 --> 00:18:30,930
complicated.

352
00:18:30,930 --> 00:18:32,753
PPO looks a little
bit more complicated

353
00:18:32,753 --> 00:18:34,670
if you look at the
gradient versus if you look

354
00:18:34,670 --> 00:18:36,132
at the surrogate objective.

355
00:18:36,132 --> 00:18:38,590
So the question is, why are we
using the importance weights

356
00:18:38,590 --> 00:18:40,270
to evaluate the gradient?

357
00:18:40,270 --> 00:18:41,950
So this importance
weight term is

358
00:18:41,950 --> 00:18:46,750
what is allowing us to
do more than one gradient

359
00:18:46,750 --> 00:18:48,030
step in the first place.

360
00:18:48,030 --> 00:18:51,630
And it's what's
allowing this blue curve

361
00:18:51,630 --> 00:18:53,702
to do better in some ways.

362
00:18:53,702 --> 00:18:55,410
If you don't have the
importance weights,

363
00:18:55,410 --> 00:19:02,230
then it's essentially
not allowing

364
00:19:02,230 --> 00:19:05,990
you to accurately
estimate the gradient

365
00:19:05,990 --> 00:19:09,590
on the old batch of data.

366
00:19:09,590 --> 00:19:14,135
So what happens when the
a network is too old?

367
00:19:14,135 --> 00:19:15,510
The advantage
estimates are going

368
00:19:15,510 --> 00:19:19,570
to be based off of
some batch of data.

369
00:19:19,570 --> 00:19:20,650
It's not like infinite.

370
00:19:20,650 --> 00:19:23,730
It's going to be a batch
of data that might be--

371
00:19:23,730 --> 00:19:27,670
I don't know, around a few
thousand time steps of data.

372
00:19:27,670 --> 00:19:32,610
And those advantage they're
also under the old policy

373
00:19:32,610 --> 00:19:34,140
before we took any
gradient steps.

374
00:19:34,140 --> 00:19:35,890
And so if you take a
lot of gradient steps

375
00:19:35,890 --> 00:19:39,010
on this objective,
those advantages

376
00:19:39,010 --> 00:19:41,370
they're only true
for the old policy.

377
00:19:41,370 --> 00:19:45,350
And they're also only based
off of a limited batch of data.

378
00:19:45,350 --> 00:19:50,370
And primarily because they were
based off of the old policy,

379
00:19:50,370 --> 00:19:52,410
after you've taken, I
don't 50 gradient steps

380
00:19:52,410 --> 00:19:55,720
or 100 gradient steps,
they become out of date.

381
00:19:55,720 --> 00:20:01,530

382
00:20:01,530 --> 00:20:05,210
In this example, it's
possible that your old policy

383
00:20:05,210 --> 00:20:09,330
right here maybe it did actually
decide to go up, for example.

384
00:20:09,330 --> 00:20:11,263
So then you learned that
if you're over here,

385
00:20:11,263 --> 00:20:12,930
you should go this
way because later you

386
00:20:12,930 --> 00:20:17,810
will go up because that's what
your advantages are telling you.

387
00:20:17,810 --> 00:20:22,107
But then in practice, once
you have your new policy,

388
00:20:22,107 --> 00:20:24,190
in the future you'll be
doing something different.

389
00:20:24,190 --> 00:20:25,570
And so those
advantages over here

390
00:20:25,570 --> 00:20:27,818
are no longer accurate,
no longer valid.

391
00:20:27,818 --> 00:20:29,610
And you need to collect
a new batch of data

392
00:20:29,610 --> 00:20:35,280
under your new policy in order
to have accurate advantages.

393
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:40,600
So the policy is going to
increase probability on actions

394
00:20:40,600 --> 00:20:42,120
with high advantages.

395
00:20:42,120 --> 00:20:44,220
These advantages are
based on the old policy,

396
00:20:44,220 --> 00:20:45,688
and so they'll get out of date.

397
00:20:45,688 --> 00:20:47,480
And so if you take a
lot of gradient steps,

398
00:20:47,480 --> 00:20:48,938
the policy is really--

399
00:20:48,938 --> 00:20:50,480
The other thing that
I didn't mention

400
00:20:50,480 --> 00:20:52,420
is that when it's
increasing the probability,

401
00:20:52,420 --> 00:20:55,400
it's also inherently trying
to differ significantly

402
00:20:55,400 --> 00:20:56,420
from the old policy.

403
00:20:56,420 --> 00:20:59,400
It's not only encouraged
to increase probability

404
00:20:59,400 --> 00:21:01,280
on advantages, but
that is going to cause

405
00:21:01,280 --> 00:21:06,200
it to change drastically
from the old policy,

406
00:21:06,200 --> 00:21:09,180
and this means because
you're also changing a lot,

407
00:21:09,180 --> 00:21:13,008
these advantages are even
less likely to be up to date

408
00:21:13,008 --> 00:21:14,050
with your current policy.

409
00:21:14,050 --> 00:21:17,080

410
00:21:17,080 --> 00:21:20,855
And so there's a question
of how do we address this.

411
00:21:20,855 --> 00:21:22,480
And there's actually
a couple of things

412
00:21:22,480 --> 00:21:26,000
that we can do in order to
actually be able to stably take

413
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,280
more gradient steps.

414
00:21:28,280 --> 00:21:31,500
And the key idea is if
we keep the new policy

415
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:34,580
to be a little bit more
close to the original policy,

416
00:21:34,580 --> 00:21:39,180
then those advantage estimates
are more likely to be valid.

417
00:21:39,180 --> 00:21:41,140
And there's two ways
in which we might

418
00:21:41,140 --> 00:21:46,580
be able to encourage
the new policy to be

419
00:21:46,580 --> 00:21:48,060
similar to the old one.

420
00:21:48,060 --> 00:21:51,780
One would be to add a term to
our surrogate objective that

421
00:21:51,780 --> 00:21:53,540
explicitly is going
to try to encourage

422
00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:55,682
the policies to be similar.

423
00:21:55,682 --> 00:21:57,140
And these policies
are representing

424
00:21:57,140 --> 00:21:58,560
distributions over actions.

425
00:21:58,560 --> 00:22:00,260
And so one way that
we could do that

426
00:22:00,260 --> 00:22:03,400
is to add a term
to our objective.

427
00:22:03,400 --> 00:22:13,140
So we have our surrogate
objective right here.

428
00:22:13,140 --> 00:22:17,540
If we have it be equal to
the same thing as what's

429
00:22:17,540 --> 00:22:26,500
written up there,
let's call this,

430
00:22:26,500 --> 00:22:31,120
I don't know, con
for constrained.

431
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:36,440
This is going to be equal to
the original surrogate objective

432
00:22:36,440 --> 00:22:38,100
that we're trying to maximize.

433
00:22:38,100 --> 00:22:41,480
And then we can
add a penalty that

434
00:22:41,480 --> 00:22:43,160
discourages you
from changing too

435
00:22:43,160 --> 00:22:45,520
much from the previous policy.

436
00:22:45,520 --> 00:22:51,720
And that penalty could take
the form of the KL divergence

437
00:22:51,720 --> 00:22:53,880
between two distributions.

438
00:22:53,880 --> 00:22:55,800
So this could be
the KL divergence

439
00:22:55,800 --> 00:23:03,680
between the new policy
distribution and the old policy

440
00:23:03,680 --> 00:23:04,700
distribution.

441
00:23:04,700 --> 00:23:08,520

442
00:23:08,520 --> 00:23:10,800
And so if we add this to our
surrogate objective, what

443
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:14,400
this will do is it'll encourage
the policies to stay close

444
00:23:14,400 --> 00:23:17,140
to one another while you
take more gradient steps,

445
00:23:17,140 --> 00:23:20,160
and so your advantage functions
will be more accurate,

446
00:23:20,160 --> 00:23:24,520
and this will allow you to more
stably take more gradient steps.

447
00:23:24,520 --> 00:23:26,660
Now, it will still be
constraining your step size.

448
00:23:26,660 --> 00:23:31,300
And so it's a little
bit of a balance.

449
00:23:31,300 --> 00:23:34,980
But essentially the results of
something like this in principle

450
00:23:34,980 --> 00:23:36,500
should look--

451
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:38,380
I'm out of colors--

452
00:23:38,380 --> 00:23:44,380
maybe should look
something like this

453
00:23:44,380 --> 00:23:49,540
if you do more gradient steps
but with this KL penalty.

454
00:23:49,540 --> 00:23:53,340
So the comment was, it's
incentivizing because

455
00:23:53,340 --> 00:23:59,400
of this ratio right here, and
it's controlling the upper part.

456
00:23:59,400 --> 00:24:00,560
The lower part is fixed.

457
00:24:00,560 --> 00:24:01,710
This is the old policy.

458
00:24:01,710 --> 00:24:02,960
It's controlling this new one.

459
00:24:02,960 --> 00:24:07,140
And so it can make this
very large or very small,

460
00:24:07,140 --> 00:24:10,920
and that will cause these
weights to either go to 0

461
00:24:10,920 --> 00:24:11,420
or become--

462
00:24:11,420 --> 00:24:14,060

463
00:24:14,060 --> 00:24:15,700
or potentially be
infinite, depending

464
00:24:15,700 --> 00:24:19,180
on how small the old
policy probability was.

465
00:24:19,180 --> 00:24:24,800
And when we constrain
how different these are,

466
00:24:24,800 --> 00:24:28,170
then we're encouraging this
to be closer to one answer.

467
00:24:28,170 --> 00:24:32,410
So you can't just
push this to infinity,

468
00:24:32,410 --> 00:24:34,690
or you can't just push it to 0.

469
00:24:34,690 --> 00:24:36,430
So a couple comments were here.

470
00:24:36,430 --> 00:24:39,270
So the first was that, to
do this scale constraint,

471
00:24:39,270 --> 00:24:43,010
we actually need to keep around
our old parameters in memory.

472
00:24:43,010 --> 00:24:46,170
This can actually
be annoying to do.

473
00:24:46,170 --> 00:24:48,030
It increases your memory costs.

474
00:24:48,030 --> 00:24:50,545
And this is actually
not uncommon to do

475
00:24:50,545 --> 00:24:51,670
with large language models.

476
00:24:51,670 --> 00:24:54,010
And you'll have to keep
around the full set of weights

477
00:24:54,010 --> 00:24:57,030
for your old language
model in order to do this.

478
00:24:57,030 --> 00:24:59,350
That said, there are ways
to potentially cache this.

479
00:24:59,350 --> 00:25:03,650
So you potentially
could just keep around

480
00:25:03,650 --> 00:25:09,250
the probabilities that were
assigned to different states

481
00:25:09,250 --> 00:25:11,410
for a given batch of data.

482
00:25:11,410 --> 00:25:13,450
And then the second
note was that,

483
00:25:13,450 --> 00:25:17,290
for this surrogate objective,
this ratio is initially

484
00:25:17,290 --> 00:25:18,070
going to be one.

485
00:25:18,070 --> 00:25:22,690

486
00:25:22,690 --> 00:25:24,650
And then once you take
one gradient step,

487
00:25:24,650 --> 00:25:28,350
that's where this
starts to diverge.

488
00:25:28,350 --> 00:25:31,870
The goal is to basically
constrain how much you're

489
00:25:31,870 --> 00:25:35,270
changing the policy and only to
change the policy a little bit

490
00:25:35,270 --> 00:25:39,535
rather than a ton.

491
00:25:39,535 --> 00:25:40,910
One other way to
think about this

492
00:25:40,910 --> 00:25:42,870
is if your policy is a
neural network that's

493
00:25:42,870 --> 00:25:45,030
taking as input like
images, for example,

494
00:25:45,030 --> 00:25:47,470
you're going to need a
lot of gradient steps

495
00:25:47,470 --> 00:25:50,350
in order to change the
features of those images.

496
00:25:50,350 --> 00:25:54,550
And so you want to be able to
take a lot of gradient steps,

497
00:25:54,550 --> 00:25:57,390
but without substantially
changing the action distribution

498
00:25:57,390 --> 00:25:59,640
that the policy is going to
take so that you can still

499
00:25:59,640 --> 00:26:03,230
refine your features without
causing this objective

500
00:26:03,230 --> 00:26:06,150
to overfit too much.

501
00:26:06,150 --> 00:26:09,910
So the question is,
how do we encourage

502
00:26:09,910 --> 00:26:12,950
it to change a little
bit, but not too much.

503
00:26:12,950 --> 00:26:14,950
Oftentimes you'll have
some hyperparameter

504
00:26:14,950 --> 00:26:18,790
that is in front of
this KL divergence

505
00:26:18,790 --> 00:26:21,670
that needs to be tuned.

506
00:26:21,670 --> 00:26:25,610
And so this beta is
like a hyperparameter.

507
00:26:25,610 --> 00:26:30,790
And this will control how much
you're allowing it to shift.

508
00:26:30,790 --> 00:26:34,210
This ends up being oftentimes a
fairly important hyperparameter

509
00:26:34,210 --> 00:26:36,730
when you use KL constraints.

510
00:26:36,730 --> 00:26:40,150
At the same time, you
don't need to tune it.

511
00:26:40,150 --> 00:26:43,330
Usually people tune it
within an order of magnitude

512
00:26:43,330 --> 00:26:48,825
rather than very carefully
finding point 4, 2, 3, whatever.

513
00:26:48,825 --> 00:26:50,450
So the question was,
how does this look

514
00:26:50,450 --> 00:26:53,450
like for the original
gradient and not quite

515
00:26:53,450 --> 00:26:54,670
understanding the surrogate?

516
00:26:54,670 --> 00:26:56,810
So the surrogate
objective is what

517
00:26:56,810 --> 00:26:59,720
we're plugging into PyTorch.

518
00:26:59,720 --> 00:27:03,330
And then PyTorch or
TensorFlow or JAX

519
00:27:03,330 --> 00:27:05,850
will then compute
the gradients for us.

520
00:27:05,850 --> 00:27:08,550
And when they compute
the gradients for us,

521
00:27:08,550 --> 00:27:09,820
they will get the first line.

522
00:27:09,820 --> 00:27:14,010

523
00:27:14,010 --> 00:27:17,790
The gradient of the second line
is equal to the first line.

524
00:27:17,790 --> 00:27:23,173

525
00:27:23,173 --> 00:27:24,590
If you were to
implement this, you

526
00:27:24,590 --> 00:27:26,173
have your original
surrogate objective

527
00:27:26,173 --> 00:27:28,450
and then you just plug-in
this term as well.

528
00:27:28,450 --> 00:27:32,110
You can look up the expression
for KL divergence between two

529
00:27:32,110 --> 00:27:34,330
categorical distributions
or two Gaussians,

530
00:27:34,330 --> 00:27:36,747
which in the Gaussian case
will be a function of the means

531
00:27:36,747 --> 00:27:37,510
and variances.

532
00:27:37,510 --> 00:27:39,790
And then just add that to
your surrogate objective

533
00:27:39,790 --> 00:27:44,030
and then have PyTorch
give you the gradients.

534
00:27:44,030 --> 00:27:47,010
If you write down the equation
for a particular distribution,

535
00:27:47,010 --> 00:27:48,790
you could also get
the gradients yourself

536
00:27:48,790 --> 00:27:51,030
if you want to take
a look at that.

537
00:27:51,030 --> 00:27:53,570
What's the difference between
J theta and J tilde theta?

538
00:27:53,570 --> 00:27:59,590
So I'm using J tilde to refer
to the surrogate objective

539
00:27:59,590 --> 00:28:01,750
because it's technically
not the true objective.

540
00:28:01,750 --> 00:28:04,710
The original J
theta objective was

541
00:28:04,710 --> 00:28:08,750
equal to the expected
sum of rewards.

542
00:28:08,750 --> 00:28:14,550
So it was the sum
of rewards here.

543
00:28:14,550 --> 00:28:16,330
So this is an
estimation of that.

544
00:28:16,330 --> 00:28:18,970

545
00:28:18,970 --> 00:28:21,900
So the first idea is
use a KL constraint.

546
00:28:21,900 --> 00:28:24,460
This is actually
very common to do.

547
00:28:24,460 --> 00:28:26,920
We will leave it
at this for now.

548
00:28:26,920 --> 00:28:29,020
We'll actually won't
use KL constraints.

549
00:28:29,020 --> 00:28:32,060
There is actually a version of
PPO that uses KL constraints,

550
00:28:32,060 --> 00:28:33,940
but it's not the
most common version.

551
00:28:33,940 --> 00:28:37,240
But it's also used a lot
in preference optimization,

552
00:28:37,240 --> 00:28:40,893
so we'll probably see it
again in a couple of weeks.

553
00:28:40,893 --> 00:28:42,560
And then there's
actually a second idea.

554
00:28:42,560 --> 00:28:45,720
So we could add this
constraint in the objective.

555
00:28:45,720 --> 00:28:49,820

556
00:28:49,820 --> 00:28:54,980
for this ratio right
here, we could simply

557
00:28:54,980 --> 00:28:58,140
remove the incentive
for the policy

558
00:28:58,140 --> 00:28:59,755
to change this ratio a lot.

559
00:28:59,755 --> 00:29:01,380
And the way that we
would do that is we

560
00:29:01,380 --> 00:29:03,820
could actually just
clip this ratio

561
00:29:03,820 --> 00:29:06,140
so that the ratio never
exceeds some value

562
00:29:06,140 --> 00:29:09,020
and never goes below some value.

563
00:29:09,020 --> 00:29:11,870
And so what that
would look like is--

564
00:29:11,870 --> 00:29:15,380

565
00:29:15,380 --> 00:29:19,960
I guess this is version 1.

566
00:29:19,960 --> 00:29:24,560
What version 2 would
look like is we

567
00:29:24,560 --> 00:29:26,680
would be taking these
importance weights,

568
00:29:26,680 --> 00:29:29,940
and we would be clipping them.

569
00:29:29,940 --> 00:29:33,560
So we have the importance
weights, pi theta prime

570
00:29:33,560 --> 00:29:36,920
divided by pi theta.

571
00:29:36,920 --> 00:29:41,560
And we'd be clipping these
to be between some lower

572
00:29:41,560 --> 00:29:45,580
value, 1 minus epsilon, and some
higher value, 1 plus epsilon.

573
00:29:45,580 --> 00:29:47,440
Epsilon could be
something like 0.2.

574
00:29:47,440 --> 00:29:52,360
So you're saying that if
it goes below 0.8, then

575
00:29:52,360 --> 00:29:53,620
it'll just be 0.8.

576
00:29:53,620 --> 00:29:56,320
And if this goes
above 1.2, we're

577
00:29:56,320 --> 00:29:59,880
just going to leave it as 1.2.

578
00:29:59,880 --> 00:30:02,520
Now, this is a
little bit informal.

579
00:30:02,520 --> 00:30:05,040
If I were to write this
out mathematically,

580
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:07,840
what this would look like.

581
00:30:07,840 --> 00:30:08,920
Let's call this w.

582
00:30:08,920 --> 00:30:10,600
This weight right here.

583
00:30:10,600 --> 00:30:16,300
Then if we wanted to clip
it below by 1 minus epsilon,

584
00:30:16,300 --> 00:30:23,660
then we would be taking the max
between w and 1 minus epsilon.

585
00:30:23,660 --> 00:30:27,060
And if we wanted to then clip
this above by 1 plus epsilon,

586
00:30:27,060 --> 00:30:34,420
then we would be taking the min
between this and 1 plus epsilon.

587
00:30:34,420 --> 00:30:38,420
And now, if we use
this clipped importance

588
00:30:38,420 --> 00:30:43,380
weights in our
objective right here,

589
00:30:43,380 --> 00:30:49,860
once the policy changes
the ratio to be above 1.2,

590
00:30:49,860 --> 00:30:52,220
it's no longer
going to be able--

591
00:30:52,220 --> 00:30:55,640
increasing the probability any
further isn't going to help it.

592
00:30:55,640 --> 00:30:57,382
It's no longer going
to be incentivized

593
00:30:57,382 --> 00:30:58,840
to increase the
probability further

594
00:30:58,840 --> 00:31:03,580
because the objective will just
stay as 1.2 times the advantage.

595
00:31:03,580 --> 00:31:05,580
And so it isn't actually
explicitly constraining

596
00:31:05,580 --> 00:31:07,580
the policy not to
change, but it's

597
00:31:07,580 --> 00:31:10,060
removing the incentive
to change significantly

598
00:31:10,060 --> 00:31:12,320
from the old policy.

599
00:31:12,320 --> 00:31:12,820
Yes.

600
00:31:12,820 --> 00:31:14,780
So it cannot be below
1 minus epsilon,

601
00:31:14,780 --> 00:31:17,770
and it cannot be
above 1 plus epsilon.

602
00:31:17,770 --> 00:31:22,210
A value of epsilon
that's used in practice

603
00:31:22,210 --> 00:31:24,370
it might be something like 0.2.

604
00:31:24,370 --> 00:31:25,730
So it's not tiny.

605
00:31:25,730 --> 00:31:28,858
Sometimes you refer to
epsilon as tiny numbers.

606
00:31:28,858 --> 00:31:30,650
In this case, it's
going to be constraining

607
00:31:30,650 --> 00:31:33,530
it to be between 0.8 and 1.2.

608
00:31:33,530 --> 00:31:38,490
So this idea of bounding
the importance weights

609
00:31:38,490 --> 00:31:42,130
is one of the key
ideas behind PPO, which

610
00:31:42,130 --> 00:31:44,890
is a popular RL algorithm.

611
00:31:44,890 --> 00:31:49,730
And so let's see.

612
00:31:49,730 --> 00:31:52,970
If we were to then put this
clipping into practice,

613
00:31:52,970 --> 00:31:54,970
we could take our
surrogate objective,

614
00:31:54,970 --> 00:31:57,370
and then replace the
importance weights

615
00:31:57,370 --> 00:31:59,450
by the clipped
importance weights

616
00:31:59,450 --> 00:32:06,130
right here in order to get a
modified surrogate objective.

617
00:32:06,130 --> 00:32:09,250
Now, there's a
couple more tricks

618
00:32:09,250 --> 00:32:15,310
that PPO introduces to try
to get stable reinforcement

619
00:32:15,310 --> 00:32:19,167
learning improvement.

620
00:32:19,167 --> 00:32:20,750
So in this first
trick, it's no longer

621
00:32:20,750 --> 00:32:23,270
incentivized to
deviate significantly.

622
00:32:23,270 --> 00:32:28,830
Now, one thing that's a little
bit funky about these importance

623
00:32:28,830 --> 00:32:31,990
weights is that there is
actually a possibility

624
00:32:31,990 --> 00:32:38,033
that it might actually--

625
00:32:38,033 --> 00:32:39,950
the clipping in some
rare cases might actually

626
00:32:39,950 --> 00:32:41,950
make the objective better.

627
00:32:41,950 --> 00:32:43,590
If, for example,
the policy ended up

628
00:32:43,590 --> 00:32:50,430
assigning a very low probability
to a negative advantage,

629
00:32:50,430 --> 00:32:52,190
and it went below
the advantages, then

630
00:32:52,190 --> 00:32:55,230
you're clipping it
to a higher weight.

631
00:32:55,230 --> 00:33:01,310
And so one thing that PPO does
and the final objective of PPO

632
00:33:01,310 --> 00:33:03,630
is to take this
clipped objective,

633
00:33:03,630 --> 00:33:05,110
but take the
minimum with respect

634
00:33:05,110 --> 00:33:07,070
to the original objective.

635
00:33:07,070 --> 00:33:09,830
And this minimum
is just making sure

636
00:33:09,830 --> 00:33:13,010
that the clipping will never
make the objective better.

637
00:33:13,010 --> 00:33:16,590

638
00:33:16,590 --> 00:33:21,330
It ensures that this is a lower
bound on the original surrogate

639
00:33:21,330 --> 00:33:23,690
objective.

640
00:33:23,690 --> 00:33:26,250
And this means that we're
going to be maximizing a lower

641
00:33:26,250 --> 00:33:27,630
bound on our objective.

642
00:33:27,630 --> 00:33:32,370
And usually, if
you're ever going

643
00:33:32,370 --> 00:33:34,330
to be optimizing or
maximizing something that's

644
00:33:34,330 --> 00:33:35,670
different from your
original objective,

645
00:33:35,670 --> 00:33:38,370
it's good if it's a lower bound
because it means that you're

646
00:33:38,370 --> 00:33:40,090
actually going to be--

647
00:33:40,090 --> 00:33:42,450
you're not going to be
maximizing something

648
00:33:42,450 --> 00:33:45,430
that you can maximize beyond
the original objective.

649
00:33:45,430 --> 00:33:48,250

650
00:33:48,250 --> 00:33:51,370
So this is the final
PPO surrogate objective.

651
00:33:51,370 --> 00:33:53,530
The motivation behind
trick 1 or trick 2?

652
00:33:53,530 --> 00:33:54,530
Trick 2.

653
00:33:54,530 --> 00:33:58,290
So the motivation
behind trick 2.

654
00:33:58,290 --> 00:34:02,070
I'm not actually sure how
often this happens in practice,

655
00:34:02,070 --> 00:34:05,330
how critical this is,
but the motivation

656
00:34:05,330 --> 00:34:08,850
is that you might
actually be in a scenario

657
00:34:08,850 --> 00:34:12,565
where maybe it increased
the probability

658
00:34:12,565 --> 00:34:13,690
on a lot of the good stuff.

659
00:34:13,690 --> 00:34:15,982
It decreased the probability
on a lot of the bad stuff.

660
00:34:15,982 --> 00:34:18,750
But in some of the rare
circumstances where

661
00:34:18,750 --> 00:34:21,270
maybe increasing the
probability on a good stuff

662
00:34:21,270 --> 00:34:23,110
led it-- there was
another advantage that

663
00:34:23,110 --> 00:34:26,750
had a similar state that
was actually really bad,

664
00:34:26,750 --> 00:34:30,670
and maybe it increased the
probability on both of those,

665
00:34:30,670 --> 00:34:34,230
in that case, clipping it
at 1.2 for that second one

666
00:34:34,230 --> 00:34:35,770
is actually making it better.

667
00:34:35,770 --> 00:34:39,052
It's actually making the
objective better because you're

668
00:34:39,052 --> 00:34:40,469
then saying, OK,
maybe it assigned

669
00:34:40,469 --> 00:34:42,830
to a really high probability
to this bad action,

670
00:34:42,830 --> 00:34:47,150
we're then going to clip that
at 1.2 or something like that.

671
00:34:47,150 --> 00:34:49,110
And so if we take the
minimum with respect

672
00:34:49,110 --> 00:34:52,130
to the original objective,
the state action level,

673
00:34:52,130 --> 00:34:54,150
at the data point
level, this is going

674
00:34:54,150 --> 00:34:58,430
to ensure that those
situations aren't happening.

675
00:34:58,430 --> 00:35:10,660
And so I guess, explicitly say
you have a particular state

676
00:35:10,660 --> 00:35:15,940
and action pair where the
advantage is, I don't know,

677
00:35:15,940 --> 00:35:21,860
negative 1000 or something like
that, so something really large.

678
00:35:21,860 --> 00:35:32,060
And say that your new policy
for this state and action

679
00:35:32,060 --> 00:35:38,740
decided to give it a probability
of 0.9 or something very high.

680
00:35:38,740 --> 00:35:44,980
And the original policy maybe
gave it a probability of--

681
00:35:44,980 --> 00:35:46,220
oops.

682
00:35:46,220 --> 00:35:51,980
If the original policy gave
it a probability of 0.1,

683
00:35:51,980 --> 00:35:56,660
then, in this case, the
ratio between these two is 9.

684
00:35:56,660 --> 00:36:01,060
And then when we clip it, it
then would be clipped to 1.2.

685
00:36:01,060 --> 00:36:03,620
And then once we
clip it to 1.2, we'll

686
00:36:03,620 --> 00:36:05,717
multiply 1.2 by negative 1000.

687
00:36:05,717 --> 00:36:08,300
And so that's actually going to
be making the objective better

688
00:36:08,300 --> 00:36:10,560
than if we hadn't
clipped the objective.

689
00:36:10,560 --> 00:36:13,320
And so what this
trick number 2 does

690
00:36:13,320 --> 00:36:16,000
is it means that
it actually doesn't

691
00:36:16,000 --> 00:36:19,560
get to do the clipping for
this particular data point.

692
00:36:19,560 --> 00:36:24,980
And this will disincentivize
it from making these accidents,

693
00:36:24,980 --> 00:36:27,600
which is a good thing to do.

694
00:36:27,600 --> 00:36:30,120
So you're asking, why don't
we just clip above and not

695
00:36:30,120 --> 00:36:31,760
clip below?

696
00:36:31,760 --> 00:36:35,440
So there is actually an
analogous version of this,

697
00:36:35,440 --> 00:36:37,360
if you just make this
negative and flip

698
00:36:37,360 --> 00:36:43,160
these, where the clipping could
also make the objective better.

699
00:36:43,160 --> 00:36:48,560
For example, if a Hertz for a
given state and action pair is

700
00:36:48,560 --> 00:36:56,200
positive 1,000, and the
new policy assigns 0.1,

701
00:36:56,200 --> 00:37:07,420
and the old policy assigns 0.9,
if you did this by accident,

702
00:37:07,420 --> 00:37:12,500
or if it happened to do this
when optimizing other data

703
00:37:12,500 --> 00:37:17,320
points, then now the ratio
is going to be 1 over 9,

704
00:37:17,320 --> 00:37:18,500
which is very small.

705
00:37:18,500 --> 00:37:21,580
You multiply that by 1,000.

706
00:37:21,580 --> 00:37:25,420
If you clip the 1 over 9
by 0.8, that's actually

707
00:37:25,420 --> 00:37:27,340
be making the objective better.

708
00:37:27,340 --> 00:37:30,500
And so generally
clipping on both sides

709
00:37:30,500 --> 00:37:36,780
and then taking the lower bound
prevents both of these scenarios

710
00:37:36,780 --> 00:37:39,150
from being encouraged
through clipping.

711
00:37:39,150 --> 00:37:44,900

712
00:37:44,900 --> 00:37:48,120
So this is the final
PPO surrogate objective.

713
00:37:48,120 --> 00:37:51,580

714
00:37:51,580 --> 00:37:56,100
The last trick that PPO does
is on estimating the advantage.

715
00:37:56,100 --> 00:38:01,180
So to estimate the
advantage, there's

716
00:38:01,180 --> 00:38:02,820
a third trick that's used.

717
00:38:02,820 --> 00:38:07,040
This is actually a
trick where I'm not sure

718
00:38:07,040 --> 00:38:09,200
how necessary it is myself.

719
00:38:09,200 --> 00:38:13,240
I haven't done a
ton of comparisons.

720
00:38:13,240 --> 00:38:16,280
And the way that it
works is you still

721
00:38:16,280 --> 00:38:19,400
can be fitting a value function
V pi in the normal way,

722
00:38:19,400 --> 00:38:22,160
either with Monte
Carlo or bootstrapping.

723
00:38:22,160 --> 00:38:25,080
But then once you fit
that value function,

724
00:38:25,080 --> 00:38:28,960
previously, the equation
for our value function

725
00:38:28,960 --> 00:38:34,560
was the reward plus the next
value minus the current value.

726
00:38:34,560 --> 00:38:40,680
And instead of just
using a horizon of 1,

727
00:38:40,680 --> 00:38:42,820
it's actually going to
use a varying horizon.

728
00:38:42,820 --> 00:38:50,520
So before we were estimating
the advantage of a given

729
00:38:50,520 --> 00:38:56,760
state and action, we're using
the reward at that state

730
00:38:56,760 --> 00:39:02,600
plus the value at the
next state minus the value

731
00:39:02,600 --> 00:39:04,140
at the current state.

732
00:39:04,140 --> 00:39:11,270
These were all for pi, and
then had hats on each of these.

733
00:39:11,270 --> 00:39:13,470
But you can also
estimate the advantage

734
00:39:13,470 --> 00:39:17,510
using something that looks like
n step returns, where you're

735
00:39:17,510 --> 00:39:21,550
actually going to be summing
over multiple of the rewards

736
00:39:21,550 --> 00:39:29,070
into the future or adding that
future value instead of adding

737
00:39:29,070 --> 00:39:30,340
just one step into the future.

738
00:39:30,340 --> 00:39:34,246

739
00:39:34,246 --> 00:39:38,390
We were talking about
bias and variance before.

740
00:39:38,390 --> 00:39:41,950
If you calculate these different
advantages with varying horizons

741
00:39:41,950 --> 00:39:44,510
and then weight
them accordingly,

742
00:39:44,510 --> 00:39:50,195
then you can get
advantage estimates that

743
00:39:50,195 --> 00:39:54,990
have varying variance
and maybe have less bias

744
00:39:54,990 --> 00:40:00,470
than if you just use the one
step estimate right here.

745
00:40:00,470 --> 00:40:02,970
I think there's some argument
in terms of bias and variance

746
00:40:02,970 --> 00:40:05,410
that can be made.

747
00:40:05,410 --> 00:40:11,670
But it's also not 100% clear if
it's necessary in my opinion.

748
00:40:11,670 --> 00:40:14,170
So there's now going to be a
hyperparameter here with regard

749
00:40:14,170 --> 00:40:16,087
to how do you choose
these weights with regard

750
00:40:16,087 --> 00:40:18,850
to the different n step returns.

751
00:40:18,850 --> 00:40:22,490

752
00:40:22,490 --> 00:40:26,290
Generally, you'll mostly
use the earlier ones

753
00:40:26,290 --> 00:40:28,770
so that you have less variance.

754
00:40:28,770 --> 00:40:33,250
And usually you will choose
it to decay very quickly

755
00:40:33,250 --> 00:40:35,310
as the horizon increases.

756
00:40:35,310 --> 00:40:37,330
So you're mostly still
going to be using

757
00:40:37,330 --> 00:40:40,958
the t equals 1 or n equals 1.

758
00:40:40,958 --> 00:40:43,250
So if you're using these
weights, do you set gamma to 1

759
00:40:43,250 --> 00:40:44,970
or do you use them together?

760
00:40:44,970 --> 00:40:46,370
You'll use them together.

761
00:40:46,370 --> 00:40:49,850
And if you write
this out, you'll

762
00:40:49,850 --> 00:40:54,210
get a term that multiplies
gamma and lambda here.

763
00:40:54,210 --> 00:40:56,730
So the question was, is this
the same as taking a weighted

764
00:40:56,730 --> 00:40:59,590
average of different n step
returns for different n's.

765
00:40:59,590 --> 00:41:01,250
So the one thing
that's different

766
00:41:01,250 --> 00:41:04,470
is we're still going to be
fitting the value function

767
00:41:04,470 --> 00:41:07,350
in the normal way, and then
this is in terms of how

768
00:41:07,350 --> 00:41:08,810
we estimate the advantage.

769
00:41:08,810 --> 00:41:12,650
But otherwise you can think
of it as n step returns,

770
00:41:12,650 --> 00:41:19,030
but averaging the different ends
in order to get an estimate.

771
00:41:19,030 --> 00:41:20,520
What's the benefit of using GAE?

772
00:41:20,520 --> 00:41:23,990

773
00:41:23,990 --> 00:41:26,250
I think it's a little
bit hard to motivate.

774
00:41:26,250 --> 00:41:29,310
I think one way to view it is
that if you just use this one

775
00:41:29,310 --> 00:41:32,470
step return, especially if
the time steps are very close

776
00:41:32,470 --> 00:41:37,830
together, then this might be--

777
00:41:37,830 --> 00:41:40,270
if your V's are not
completely accurate,

778
00:41:40,270 --> 00:41:42,650
then this estimate
might be a bit off.

779
00:41:42,650 --> 00:41:44,590
And if you increase
this horizon,

780
00:41:44,590 --> 00:41:48,110
then you'll get potentially
a better estimate.

781
00:41:48,110 --> 00:41:52,205
Now, why not just use one
value of n versus a mixture?

782
00:41:52,205 --> 00:41:53,830
Perhaps it helps you
hedge a little bit

783
00:41:53,830 --> 00:41:56,350
if you're not sure which
value of n is correct.

784
00:41:56,350 --> 00:42:03,160
But I don't have a great
motivation myself for it.

785
00:42:03,160 --> 00:42:04,700
If you use it and
don't use it, I'm

786
00:42:04,700 --> 00:42:07,120
not 100% sure what the
performance is like.

787
00:42:07,120 --> 00:42:10,280

788
00:42:10,280 --> 00:42:12,140
So the final PPO algorithm.

789
00:42:12,140 --> 00:42:13,640
We sample a batch of data.

790
00:42:13,640 --> 00:42:17,260
We fit a value
function to that data,

791
00:42:17,260 --> 00:42:21,300
and then we evaluate this
generalized advantage estimate

792
00:42:21,300 --> 00:42:23,100
that we just talked about.

793
00:42:23,100 --> 00:42:25,540
And then we will
update the policy

794
00:42:25,540 --> 00:42:29,403
by taking M gradient steps
on your surrogate objective

795
00:42:29,403 --> 00:42:30,820
where the surrogate
objective uses

796
00:42:30,820 --> 00:42:35,700
these clipped importance
weights using the GAE estimate

797
00:42:35,700 --> 00:42:38,980
of the advantage function.

798
00:42:38,980 --> 00:42:40,860
And then you can
repeat this process

799
00:42:40,860 --> 00:42:44,720
after taking M gradient steps,
then sample a new batch of data,

800
00:42:44,720 --> 00:42:47,210
fit a new value function to
that, and repeat the process.

801
00:42:47,210 --> 00:42:50,940

802
00:42:50,940 --> 00:42:55,000
In order to get a rough sense
for what this looks like,

803
00:42:55,000 --> 00:42:56,587
here are some example
hyperparameters.

804
00:42:56,587 --> 00:42:58,420
Now, generally
hyperparameters are something

805
00:42:58,420 --> 00:43:00,020
that you would tune per domain.

806
00:43:00,020 --> 00:43:02,368
So this is not like my
recommended hyperparameters

807
00:43:02,368 --> 00:43:04,160
necessarily, but just
to give a rough sense

808
00:43:04,160 --> 00:43:05,840
for what this looks like.

809
00:43:05,840 --> 00:43:09,120
The batch of data might have
around 2000 time steps in it.

810
00:43:09,120 --> 00:43:13,640
So if you have a long horizon
problem that has 1,000 time

811
00:43:13,640 --> 00:43:16,260
steps, this might actually be
only a couple trajectories.

812
00:43:16,260 --> 00:43:17,843
If you have a shorter
horizon problem,

813
00:43:17,843 --> 00:43:20,480
this might be more trajectories.

814
00:43:20,480 --> 00:43:24,880
When you update the
policy on step 4,

815
00:43:24,880 --> 00:43:26,440
a common hyperparameter
is something

816
00:43:26,440 --> 00:43:31,960
around 10 epochs on the data set
that was collected in step 1.

817
00:43:31,960 --> 00:43:35,560
And if your batch
size is 64, this

818
00:43:35,560 --> 00:43:38,160
might be around
300 gradient steps.

819
00:43:38,160 --> 00:43:41,720
And so this means that
in comparison to before,

820
00:43:41,720 --> 00:43:43,460
because we introduced
this clipping,

821
00:43:43,460 --> 00:43:48,640
we're actually able to take 300
gradient steps without having

822
00:43:48,640 --> 00:43:51,400
really unstable improvement.

823
00:43:51,400 --> 00:43:54,255
So that's good.

824
00:43:54,255 --> 00:43:54,880
Clipping range.

825
00:43:54,880 --> 00:43:58,620
I mentioned 0.2 is something
that might be reasonable.

826
00:43:58,620 --> 00:44:05,700
And then you might run this
full outer loop 500 times

827
00:44:05,700 --> 00:44:09,000
in order to get around a million
total time steps of experience.

828
00:44:09,000 --> 00:44:13,880

829
00:44:13,880 --> 00:44:16,397
We'll see some other examples,
actually some cooler examples

830
00:44:16,397 --> 00:44:17,480
at the end of the lecture.

831
00:44:17,480 --> 00:44:21,300
But if you run PPO
on this very simple

832
00:44:21,300 --> 00:44:27,486
simulated the right half
of a cheetah or something,

833
00:44:27,486 --> 00:44:31,320
and you look at, as you collect
more time steps of data,

834
00:44:31,320 --> 00:44:33,820
what does the reward look like?

835
00:44:33,820 --> 00:44:35,540
There are a number
of algorithms here.

836
00:44:35,540 --> 00:44:37,982
This beige one is
vanilla policy gradient.

837
00:44:37,982 --> 00:44:40,440
It's a slightly smarter version
of vanilla policy gradient,

838
00:44:40,440 --> 00:44:42,700
but it's basically vanilla
policy gradient where you're not

839
00:44:42,700 --> 00:44:44,617
fitting the advantage
function, and you're not

840
00:44:44,617 --> 00:44:45,540
doing the clipping.

841
00:44:45,540 --> 00:44:47,700
And then PPO is up here.

842
00:44:47,700 --> 00:44:51,540
And you can see that with less
experience able to improve

843
00:44:51,540 --> 00:44:56,300
much faster because it's
fitting the advantage function,

844
00:44:56,300 --> 00:44:59,080
and because it's doing the
clipping on the gradient

845
00:44:59,080 --> 00:45:04,680
in order to take many more
gradient steps on a single data

846
00:45:04,680 --> 00:45:06,340
point.

847
00:45:06,340 --> 00:45:08,280
So can I explain the
error from 4 to 1

848
00:45:08,280 --> 00:45:10,340
and how are the
parameters being updated?

849
00:45:10,340 --> 00:45:13,318
So this error is just saying
that after we finish step four,

850
00:45:13,318 --> 00:45:15,360
then we're going to go
back to step 1 and collect

851
00:45:15,360 --> 00:45:16,920
a new batch of data.

852
00:45:16,920 --> 00:45:21,640
When we do that,
we're going to keep

853
00:45:21,640 --> 00:45:26,200
our policy as it was at
the current time step

854
00:45:26,200 --> 00:45:28,380
and keep on iterating.

855
00:45:28,380 --> 00:45:30,160
And so at this
point actually theta

856
00:45:30,160 --> 00:45:33,200
will become the
previous theta prime,

857
00:45:33,200 --> 00:45:38,280
and you'll be sampling a batch
of data from your latest policy.

858
00:45:38,280 --> 00:45:42,080
And then the other thing here is
that, for this value function,

859
00:45:42,080 --> 00:45:44,540
you could warm started at
the previous value function

860
00:45:44,540 --> 00:45:46,240
or you could train
it from scratch.

861
00:45:46,240 --> 00:45:48,467
It depends a little
bit on the domain.

862
00:45:48,467 --> 00:45:50,300
So in terms of these
plots, the question is,

863
00:45:50,300 --> 00:45:54,430
do you fit the best set of
hyperparameters and then run it?

864
00:45:54,430 --> 00:45:57,450
So each of them will have its
own set of hyperparameters.

865
00:45:57,450 --> 00:46:01,470
And the best practice is to
figure out good hyperparameters

866
00:46:01,470 --> 00:46:04,250
on one environment or
maybe two environments,

867
00:46:04,250 --> 00:46:06,350
and then apply those
hyperparameters

868
00:46:06,350 --> 00:46:09,292
to the remaining environments
in your experiments.

869
00:46:09,292 --> 00:46:10,750
There are scenarios
where you might

870
00:46:10,750 --> 00:46:12,125
need a new set of
hyperparameters

871
00:46:12,125 --> 00:46:13,370
for a different domain.

872
00:46:13,370 --> 00:46:15,990
For example, if you are
controlling something

873
00:46:15,990 --> 00:46:20,070
at 1 Hertz versus 5 Hertz,
or optimizing a language

874
00:46:20,070 --> 00:46:22,790
model versus a half
cheetah, the hyperparameters

875
00:46:22,790 --> 00:46:23,870
will be different.

876
00:46:23,870 --> 00:46:26,130
So there is some
variability there.

877
00:46:26,130 --> 00:46:28,742

878
00:46:28,742 --> 00:46:30,950
One good practice is to not
tune your hyperparameters

879
00:46:30,950 --> 00:46:32,908
per environment because
then you're essentially

880
00:46:32,908 --> 00:46:35,810
using more data,
more samples in order

881
00:46:35,810 --> 00:46:37,810
to get those hyperparameters
in the first place,

882
00:46:37,810 --> 00:46:40,530
and that wouldn't be reflected
on this curve right here.

883
00:46:40,530 --> 00:46:43,136

884
00:46:43,136 --> 00:46:44,910
We should move on.

885
00:46:44,910 --> 00:46:47,710
But happy to answer
it after class.

886
00:46:47,710 --> 00:46:51,670
So far we've talked about
using one batch of data

887
00:46:51,670 --> 00:46:54,247
for one gradient step that's
the fully on policy version.

888
00:46:54,247 --> 00:46:56,330
And we've looked at using
one batch of policy data

889
00:46:56,330 --> 00:46:57,910
for multiple gradient steps.

890
00:46:57,910 --> 00:47:01,278
And this is off-policy.

891
00:47:01,278 --> 00:47:03,570
What I'd like to talk about
for the rest of the lecture

892
00:47:03,570 --> 00:47:06,450
is can we be even
more off-policy.

893
00:47:06,450 --> 00:47:09,450
So can we reuse
previous batches of data

894
00:47:09,450 --> 00:47:12,610
rather than recollecting
a new batch each time,

895
00:47:12,610 --> 00:47:15,850
like all of the past trial
and error experience.

896
00:47:15,850 --> 00:47:17,790
And there's two
ideas for doing this.

897
00:47:17,790 --> 00:47:20,900
One is that we actually need
to retain all of our past data.

898
00:47:20,900 --> 00:47:23,570

899
00:47:23,570 --> 00:47:26,090
We'll use a term called a
replay buffer that you actually

900
00:47:26,090 --> 00:47:32,270
saw in homework 1, where we will
have a data set of past data.

901
00:47:32,270 --> 00:47:33,810
We'll keep on adding to it.

902
00:47:33,810 --> 00:47:36,730
And at some point, we might
also remove some very old data

903
00:47:36,730 --> 00:47:37,478
from it.

904
00:47:37,478 --> 00:47:39,770
This is called a replay buffer
because you're replaying

905
00:47:39,770 --> 00:47:42,425
your past experiences.

906
00:47:42,425 --> 00:47:44,050
And then the second
idea is we actually

907
00:47:44,050 --> 00:47:45,550
need to adjust some
of the equations

908
00:47:45,550 --> 00:47:47,530
to remove some on
policy assumptions

909
00:47:47,530 --> 00:47:51,510
in order to be able to actually
use all of our past data.

910
00:47:51,510 --> 00:47:54,450

911
00:47:54,450 --> 00:47:57,590
So what this looks like is we'll
take our actor critic algorithm.

912
00:47:57,590 --> 00:48:00,550
But to make it off-policy,
we can think about it

913
00:48:00,550 --> 00:48:04,032
as getting one time step of
data, then updating our policy,

914
00:48:04,032 --> 00:48:05,990
getting another time
step, updating our policy,

915
00:48:05,990 --> 00:48:06,610
and so forth.

916
00:48:06,610 --> 00:48:09,490
So rather than getting one
batch and then doing updates,

917
00:48:09,490 --> 00:48:13,030
we can actually do
this much more online.

918
00:48:13,030 --> 00:48:15,270
And when we update our
policy, this will actually

919
00:48:15,270 --> 00:48:17,750
be coming from a replay buffer.

920
00:48:17,750 --> 00:48:19,790
We'll be actually
doing gradient steps

921
00:48:19,790 --> 00:48:23,230
using all of our
past experiences

922
00:48:23,230 --> 00:48:25,770
rather than just our
immediate past experience.

923
00:48:25,770 --> 00:48:30,655

924
00:48:30,655 --> 00:48:32,030
When we collect
experience, we'll

925
00:48:32,030 --> 00:48:34,590
want to then add this
to our replay buffer.

926
00:48:34,590 --> 00:48:37,810
And when we then
update our policy,

927
00:48:37,810 --> 00:48:40,790
we want to use a minibatch
sampled from all previous data

928
00:48:40,790 --> 00:48:43,710
rather than just our
last batch of data.

929
00:48:43,710 --> 00:48:46,790
So that's the modifications
we need to make in order

930
00:48:46,790 --> 00:48:49,710
to use a replay buffer.

931
00:48:49,710 --> 00:48:52,970
So walking through this, we
start with an empty replay

932
00:48:52,970 --> 00:48:54,170
buffer.

933
00:48:54,170 --> 00:48:57,810
We then collect experience
from our policy.

934
00:48:57,810 --> 00:49:00,330
We add that to
the replay buffer,

935
00:49:00,330 --> 00:49:04,490
and then we sample a
transition state action

936
00:49:04,490 --> 00:49:05,850
reward and next state.

937
00:49:05,850 --> 00:49:08,730
From the replay buffer,
there's a small probability

938
00:49:08,730 --> 00:49:11,130
this could be exactly
what we just sampled,

939
00:49:11,130 --> 00:49:13,450
but otherwise it'll be
something randomly sampled

940
00:49:13,450 --> 00:49:15,970
from our previous experience.

941
00:49:15,970 --> 00:49:20,090
Then we'll update our value
function using the batch of data

942
00:49:20,090 --> 00:49:23,330
that we just sampled
from the buffer.

943
00:49:23,330 --> 00:49:26,870
We can update our value function
with the same objective before

944
00:49:26,870 --> 00:49:31,730
but now we're going to be
averaging over our minibatch.

945
00:49:31,730 --> 00:49:34,050
And then we can
evaluate the advantage,

946
00:49:34,050 --> 00:49:39,530
and then take a gradient
step on our policy.

947
00:49:39,530 --> 00:49:41,470
So that's the mechanics
of replay buffers.

948
00:49:41,470 --> 00:49:46,130

949
00:49:46,130 --> 00:49:49,880
Finally update the policy
and then repeat this process.

950
00:49:49,880 --> 00:49:54,800
Unfortunately, this algorithm
right now is currently broken.

951
00:49:54,800 --> 00:49:56,760
And it's broken in
two different ways.

952
00:49:56,760 --> 00:49:58,940
The first way that's
broken is right here.

953
00:49:58,940 --> 00:50:03,240
So say that we're
using bootstrapping

954
00:50:03,240 --> 00:50:05,880
to update our value
function, then

955
00:50:05,880 --> 00:50:08,920
our target would be the reward
at the current time step

956
00:50:08,920 --> 00:50:14,760
plus our value estimate
for the next state.

957
00:50:14,760 --> 00:50:17,040
And so I have a
question for you.

958
00:50:17,040 --> 00:50:19,920
And the question is, when
we're fitting a value function

959
00:50:19,920 --> 00:50:21,820
on all of the data
in the replay buffer,

960
00:50:21,820 --> 00:50:25,658
so this is the data from all
of our previous policies,

961
00:50:25,658 --> 00:50:28,200
the question is, what policy
are we learning a value function

962
00:50:28,200 --> 00:50:32,240
for when we fit the value
function this way from data

963
00:50:32,240 --> 00:50:33,680
in a replay buffer?

964
00:50:33,680 --> 00:50:35,720
And so I'll give you two
minutes to discuss that

965
00:50:35,720 --> 00:50:36,780
and then we'll come back.

966
00:50:36,780 --> 00:50:42,200

967
00:50:42,200 --> 00:50:43,983
Let's come back.

968
00:50:43,983 --> 00:50:45,400
I also should have
maybe mentioned

969
00:50:45,400 --> 00:50:51,380
that the answer is not as
clean as some other questions.

970
00:50:51,380 --> 00:50:54,420
So the response there was the--

971
00:50:54,420 --> 00:50:58,100
so for our latest policy,
we actually don't have any--

972
00:50:58,100 --> 00:51:02,980
we have maybe one data
point from that policy.

973
00:51:02,980 --> 00:51:05,080
the rest of the buffer
is not from that policy.

974
00:51:05,080 --> 00:51:08,220
And so we don't actually have
any really much data at all

975
00:51:08,220 --> 00:51:09,268
from our current policy.

976
00:51:09,268 --> 00:51:10,560
So it's not our current policy.

977
00:51:10,560 --> 00:51:12,600
And that's why
this is incorrect.

978
00:51:12,600 --> 00:51:16,540
And in particular, when
we collect trajectories

979
00:51:16,540 --> 00:51:20,720
from previous policies, the
actions will be different,

980
00:51:20,720 --> 00:51:22,660
and that means that the
next state will also

981
00:51:22,660 --> 00:51:26,850
be different when you took that
action as well as the reward.

982
00:51:26,850 --> 00:51:29,100
So the policy that you're
going to be learning a value

983
00:51:29,100 --> 00:51:30,540
function for--

984
00:51:30,540 --> 00:51:32,040
I'll phrase it
slightly differently.

985
00:51:32,040 --> 00:51:34,332
The policy that you'll be
learning a value function for

986
00:51:34,332 --> 00:51:37,870
is some mixture of all
of your past policies.

987
00:51:37,870 --> 00:51:42,090

988
00:51:42,090 --> 00:51:46,720
It's actually not
trivial to write it down.

989
00:51:46,720 --> 00:51:49,760
If things were collected
in a particular way,

990
00:51:49,760 --> 00:51:54,920
the policy might look
something like an average

991
00:51:54,920 --> 00:52:01,195
over your previous policies,
if you had N previous policies.

992
00:52:01,195 --> 00:52:03,320
Now, it's actually a little
bit different than this

993
00:52:03,320 --> 00:52:06,080
because in some
states one policy

994
00:52:06,080 --> 00:52:08,640
might have gone over
here, and another policy

995
00:52:08,640 --> 00:52:10,640
might have gone over here.

996
00:52:10,640 --> 00:52:12,320
And so then in this
state, it's going

997
00:52:12,320 --> 00:52:13,487
to be a mixture of policies.

998
00:52:13,487 --> 00:52:15,980
But in this state, it's going
to be more of this policy.

999
00:52:15,980 --> 00:52:18,080
In this state it's going
to be a different policy.

1000
00:52:18,080 --> 00:52:20,120
So it's actually a
little bit messy.

1001
00:52:20,120 --> 00:52:22,440
But the thing
that's definitive is

1002
00:52:22,440 --> 00:52:24,240
that it's not going
to be a value function

1003
00:52:24,240 --> 00:52:26,160
for your current policy.

1004
00:52:26,160 --> 00:52:30,560
And we would like to get a value
function for our current policy

1005
00:52:30,560 --> 00:52:33,120
using data from
previous policies

1006
00:52:33,120 --> 00:52:35,880
so that we can actually
estimate this advantage

1007
00:52:35,880 --> 00:52:38,560
for our current policy and
get an accurate gradient

1008
00:52:38,560 --> 00:52:41,305
for our current policy.

1009
00:52:41,305 --> 00:52:42,680
It's a little bit
messy, but it's

1010
00:52:42,680 --> 00:52:46,820
a policy that is
a conglomeration

1011
00:52:46,820 --> 00:52:50,980
of your previous
policies or some mixture

1012
00:52:50,980 --> 00:52:52,360
of your previous policies.

1013
00:52:52,360 --> 00:52:55,260
It's not something that you
can very cleanly write down,

1014
00:52:55,260 --> 00:52:57,390
but it's a mixture
of your past policy.

1015
00:52:57,390 --> 00:53:01,300

1016
00:53:01,300 --> 00:53:04,260
The other thing that's
a bit wrong about this

1017
00:53:04,260 --> 00:53:06,980
is that here this is
also not the action

1018
00:53:06,980 --> 00:53:09,140
that the policy
would have taken.

1019
00:53:09,140 --> 00:53:11,680
And likewise, when you pass
the action into the advantage,

1020
00:53:11,680 --> 00:53:14,980
that's also not the action that
the policy would have taken.

1021
00:53:14,980 --> 00:53:18,162
And so we'll also see
how we can fix that part.

1022
00:53:18,162 --> 00:53:20,120
But first, let's talk
about the value function.

1023
00:53:20,120 --> 00:53:21,495
And we have this
key question of,

1024
00:53:21,495 --> 00:53:24,780
how do we fit a value function
for our current policy using

1025
00:53:24,780 --> 00:53:27,390
a replay buffer of data
from past policies?

1026
00:53:27,390 --> 00:53:30,420

1027
00:53:30,420 --> 00:53:37,820
So one idea here is,
instead of trying to fit V,

1028
00:53:37,820 --> 00:53:42,180
what if we try to
fit Q instead of V?

1029
00:53:42,180 --> 00:53:50,230
So we can remember
that the Q function

1030
00:53:50,230 --> 00:53:52,250
it's very similar to
the value function,

1031
00:53:52,250 --> 00:53:57,530
but it's the sum of rewards if
we start at s and take action a.

1032
00:53:57,530 --> 00:54:04,850
So you can think of it as the
sum over say, this is St of at.

1033
00:54:04,850 --> 00:54:08,770
This is the sum of time
steps starting at t

1034
00:54:08,770 --> 00:54:15,650
through the end of the
trajectory of the reward m

1035
00:54:15,650 --> 00:54:23,010
r of St prime at prime under
the expectation that we're

1036
00:54:23,010 --> 00:54:28,650
running our policies after
taking state St and action at.

1037
00:54:28,650 --> 00:54:33,290
And so this under the
expectation of pi theta

1038
00:54:33,290 --> 00:54:39,410
conditioned on St, at.

1039
00:54:39,410 --> 00:54:42,380
So this is just the
definition of the Q function.

1040
00:54:42,380 --> 00:54:48,230

1041
00:54:48,230 --> 00:54:51,170
So we have the summation
over future time steps.

1042
00:54:51,170 --> 00:54:53,910

1043
00:54:53,910 --> 00:54:56,470
We can start to write
out the summation.

1044
00:54:56,470 --> 00:55:01,790
And in particular, if we
write out the first time step,

1045
00:55:01,790 --> 00:55:03,820
the first state and action
is actually just St,

1046
00:55:03,820 --> 00:55:07,110
at because that's what we're
passing into the Q function.

1047
00:55:07,110 --> 00:55:12,110
So we can write this as, we know
the reward of the first state

1048
00:55:12,110 --> 00:55:14,150
and action that we
took, and then we

1049
00:55:14,150 --> 00:55:20,870
could have a summation over
things starting at t plus 1

1050
00:55:20,870 --> 00:55:24,120
of the sum of future rewards.

1051
00:55:24,120 --> 00:55:27,310

1052
00:55:27,310 --> 00:55:29,370
Now, for this sum
of future rewards,

1053
00:55:29,370 --> 00:55:35,270
this would now need to be
an expectation after St, at.

1054
00:55:35,270 --> 00:55:39,150
And so for this
one, we would need

1055
00:55:39,150 --> 00:55:47,610
an expectation if we were
starting from St plus 1

1056
00:55:47,610 --> 00:55:57,250
under the dynamics, and
remaining actions and states

1057
00:55:57,250 --> 00:56:00,930
sampled from pi theta.

1058
00:56:00,930 --> 00:56:03,770
This is for pi theta.

1059
00:56:03,770 --> 00:56:09,810
Now, this is starting to look
also a lot like a value function

1060
00:56:09,810 --> 00:56:16,610
but for the St plus 1.

1061
00:56:16,610 --> 00:56:21,610
And so you can actually also
write this in terms of Q.

1062
00:56:21,610 --> 00:56:24,810
And in particular,
you can write this

1063
00:56:24,810 --> 00:56:32,330
is equivalent to the reward at
the current time step plus--

1064
00:56:32,330 --> 00:56:35,310
if we then try to take
this one step further,

1065
00:56:35,310 --> 00:56:37,990
then we'll have St plus 1
sampled by the dynamics,

1066
00:56:37,990 --> 00:56:40,850
and at plus 1 sampled
from our policy.

1067
00:56:40,850 --> 00:56:43,800

1068
00:56:43,800 --> 00:56:48,680
So if we have the
expectation over St plus 1

1069
00:56:48,680 --> 00:56:53,880
and at plus 1 sampled
from our policy,

1070
00:56:53,880 --> 00:56:55,720
then the Q at the
current time step

1071
00:56:55,720 --> 00:57:03,960
is the reward plus this
expectation of the next Q.

1072
00:57:03,960 --> 00:57:09,120
And so we can essentially
define Q recursively

1073
00:57:09,120 --> 00:57:11,080
like this as the reward
at the current time

1074
00:57:11,080 --> 00:57:14,960
step plus the future Q
at the next time step.

1075
00:57:14,960 --> 00:57:18,120
And the thing that's really
nice about this equation

1076
00:57:18,120 --> 00:57:23,080
is that this equation holds
for any state in action.

1077
00:57:23,080 --> 00:57:26,480
We haven't made any assumption
about which state and action

1078
00:57:26,480 --> 00:57:28,860
the data was collected for.

1079
00:57:28,860 --> 00:57:31,440

1080
00:57:31,440 --> 00:57:33,700
And so if we walk through
that on the slides again.

1081
00:57:33,700 --> 00:57:38,200
So we have data points that are
state action reward next state.

1082
00:57:38,200 --> 00:57:40,140
This is sampled from
some other policy,

1083
00:57:40,140 --> 00:57:41,920
and we also have
the future reward.

1084
00:57:41,920 --> 00:57:46,900
This is a trajectory
collected from an old policy.

1085
00:57:46,900 --> 00:57:49,580
This action was taken
from the past policy.

1086
00:57:49,580 --> 00:57:53,980
And this future reward depends
on the past policies actions.

1087
00:57:53,980 --> 00:57:56,700
We don't want to use the
future reward part because this

1088
00:57:56,700 --> 00:57:59,100
is going to depend on the past
policy actions in a pretty

1089
00:57:59,100 --> 00:58:01,980
complicated way.

1090
00:58:01,980 --> 00:58:05,580
But if we're fitting
Q of s, a, then we're

1091
00:58:05,580 --> 00:58:07,480
actually passing
the action as input,

1092
00:58:07,480 --> 00:58:09,195
and it's actually
OK if that action

1093
00:58:09,195 --> 00:58:11,570
is a bit different from what
our policy would have taken.

1094
00:58:11,570 --> 00:58:14,260

1095
00:58:14,260 --> 00:58:18,180
And so because that's
OK, and because we

1096
00:58:18,180 --> 00:58:22,360
have this equation that
represents this recursion,

1097
00:58:22,360 --> 00:58:24,460
then we can actually
try to fit Qa

1098
00:58:24,460 --> 00:58:26,400
to a target that
looks like this.

1099
00:58:26,400 --> 00:58:28,980

1100
00:58:28,980 --> 00:58:31,260
And so we have our
definition of the Q function

1101
00:58:31,260 --> 00:58:32,700
of the future
rewards conditioned

1102
00:58:32,700 --> 00:58:34,060
on the state and action.

1103
00:58:34,060 --> 00:58:36,880
And for any state and action,
we have this equivalence

1104
00:58:36,880 --> 00:58:41,800
where Q for pi theta of s, a is
the reward at the current time

1105
00:58:41,800 --> 00:58:46,720
step plus the Q function
at the next time step

1106
00:58:46,720 --> 00:58:49,360
where you have an expectation
over the next time step,

1107
00:58:49,360 --> 00:58:53,500
and an expectation over the
next action for a policy.

1108
00:58:53,500 --> 00:58:54,000
Yeah.

1109
00:58:54,000 --> 00:58:54,583
Good question.

1110
00:58:54,583 --> 00:59:00,660
So the question was, for this
data, we have this action.

1111
00:59:00,660 --> 00:59:03,480
And for the Q function,
it takes as input

1112
00:59:03,480 --> 00:59:07,200
an action but the
rest of the trajectory

1113
00:59:07,200 --> 00:59:08,900
was still following
a different policy.

1114
00:59:08,900 --> 00:59:11,720
And the Q needs to
be like-- we need

1115
00:59:11,720 --> 00:59:13,820
to be summing the rewards
for a current policy

1116
00:59:13,820 --> 00:59:15,303
not the other policy.

1117
00:59:15,303 --> 00:59:16,720
And the thing that
we've done here

1118
00:59:16,720 --> 00:59:21,760
is that if we represent
this Q in terms

1119
00:59:21,760 --> 00:59:27,000
of Q for our current policy,
this second term right here

1120
00:59:27,000 --> 00:59:30,060
is actually not using
the future data at all.

1121
00:59:30,060 --> 00:59:32,920

1122
00:59:32,920 --> 00:59:37,340
Well, the S prime we
will be using the data,

1123
00:59:37,340 --> 00:59:41,260
but it's not using
anything after S prime.

1124
00:59:41,260 --> 00:59:47,460
And in particular, we're using
our bootstrap estimate of Q

1125
00:59:47,460 --> 00:59:49,340
at the next time step.

1126
00:59:49,340 --> 00:59:53,980
And for the next action,
instead of using the action that

1127
00:59:53,980 --> 00:59:57,500
was taken in our data, we
can use the action sampled

1128
00:59:57,500 --> 00:59:58,830
from our current policy.

1129
00:59:58,830 --> 01:00:01,340

1130
01:00:01,340 --> 01:00:04,620
So we're recursively
defining the Q function here,

1131
01:00:04,620 --> 01:00:09,140
and the only things
that will-- maybe we

1132
01:00:09,140 --> 01:00:11,320
can actually just go
through the next part.

1133
01:00:11,320 --> 01:00:14,380
So what we'll do here is
we'll sample an s, a, s

1134
01:00:14,380 --> 01:00:15,640
prime from the buffer.

1135
01:00:15,640 --> 01:00:20,420
This a was from an old policy,
but the dynamics are constant.

1136
01:00:20,420 --> 01:00:25,020
And then we'll sample an a
prime from our current policy.

1137
01:00:25,020 --> 01:00:31,580
This is sampled from our current
policy conditioned on s prime.

1138
01:00:31,580 --> 01:00:35,010
And then we can
estimate the q function

1139
01:00:35,010 --> 01:00:37,170
as the reward at the
current time step

1140
01:00:37,170 --> 01:00:42,690
plus the q function for the
next state in the buffer,

1141
01:00:42,690 --> 01:00:45,910
and an action sampled
from our policy here.

1142
01:00:45,910 --> 01:00:48,330
And because this action
is sampled from our policy

1143
01:00:48,330 --> 01:00:52,170
rather than from
the data set, then

1144
01:00:52,170 --> 01:00:54,090
we're actually
getting an estimate

1145
01:00:54,090 --> 01:00:56,930
for our current
policies Q function.

1146
01:00:56,930 --> 01:00:57,430
So yeah.

1147
01:00:57,430 --> 01:00:57,910
Good question.

1148
01:00:57,910 --> 01:00:59,493
So the question was,
does this require

1149
01:00:59,493 --> 01:01:01,880
that we see data from all
possible state action pairs?

1150
01:01:01,880 --> 01:01:04,510

1151
01:01:04,510 --> 01:01:07,370
There is a very
good insight there.

1152
01:01:07,370 --> 01:01:10,250
We don't actually need to see
data from all state action

1153
01:01:10,250 --> 01:01:13,390
pairs, but it means that, for
this target to be accurate,

1154
01:01:13,390 --> 01:01:16,210
we do need to have
data that covers

1155
01:01:16,210 --> 01:01:19,330
actions that are similar to
what our policy would be taking.

1156
01:01:19,330 --> 01:01:21,230
And if we haven't
seen that data,

1157
01:01:21,230 --> 01:01:24,550
this Q function is
going to be inaccurate.

1158
01:01:24,550 --> 01:01:28,850
And you'll need to collect
data from your current policy

1159
01:01:28,850 --> 01:01:29,850
in order to get that.

1160
01:01:29,850 --> 01:01:33,150

1161
01:01:33,150 --> 01:01:36,070
We're not getting a really
good estimate of our Q function

1162
01:01:36,070 --> 01:01:37,690
for free based on past policies.

1163
01:01:37,690 --> 01:01:41,070
We still need decent coverage of
what our policy would have done,

1164
01:01:41,070 --> 01:01:46,630
but it provides somewhat
of a means to use old data.

1165
01:01:46,630 --> 01:01:49,350
And so I guess just
to finish the slide

1166
01:01:49,350 --> 01:01:51,070
and then come back
to that point,

1167
01:01:51,070 --> 01:01:57,110
we'll be using r plus
gamma times Q as our label.

1168
01:01:57,110 --> 01:01:58,870
And then we can use
supervised learning

1169
01:01:58,870 --> 01:02:01,750
to pass as input to the state
and action to a neural network,

1170
01:02:01,750 --> 01:02:06,510
and then try to output
this label right here.

1171
01:02:06,510 --> 01:02:09,510
And again, we can
use a similar like L2

1172
01:02:09,510 --> 01:02:12,490
error between our
prediction and the target,

1173
01:02:12,490 --> 01:02:15,070
just like with the
value function.

1174
01:02:15,070 --> 01:02:18,070
And to be accurate,
these targets

1175
01:02:18,070 --> 01:02:21,750
need to have sufficient action
coverage and need to have--

1176
01:02:21,750 --> 01:02:23,470
the action taken
from your policy

1177
01:02:23,470 --> 01:02:25,330
can't be wildly out
of distribution,

1178
01:02:25,330 --> 01:02:28,030
otherwise these targets
will be inaccurate.

1179
01:02:28,030 --> 01:02:28,530
Yeah.

1180
01:02:28,530 --> 01:02:31,190
So we'll need to have taken
that action from that state.

1181
01:02:31,190 --> 01:02:37,670
And so this does
make it, if you don't

1182
01:02:37,670 --> 01:02:39,670
have any data from that
state, that's a problem.

1183
01:02:39,670 --> 01:02:41,378
And then if you have
data from that state

1184
01:02:41,378 --> 01:02:45,582
but only for very different
actions is also a problem.

1185
01:02:45,582 --> 01:02:47,530
Some of this is also
going to be relying on,

1186
01:02:47,530 --> 01:02:51,550
neural networks can generalize,
can interpolate reasonably well,

1187
01:02:51,550 --> 01:02:53,230
and so that helps this.

1188
01:02:53,230 --> 01:02:55,330
But you do need sufficient
coverage for this

1189
01:02:55,330 --> 01:02:57,210
to be accurate.

1190
01:02:57,210 --> 01:03:02,350
So we actually didn't quite get
to this equation on the board.

1191
01:03:02,350 --> 01:03:04,810
We have this expectation
here over the next state

1192
01:03:04,810 --> 01:03:07,370
and an action sampled
from our policy.

1193
01:03:07,370 --> 01:03:11,650
And this equation estimates
this with a single sample

1194
01:03:11,650 --> 01:03:18,790
where we have St and at.

1195
01:03:18,790 --> 01:03:20,690
And then we're going
to use the St plus 1

1196
01:03:20,690 --> 01:03:23,330
from our data set,
which is just one

1197
01:03:23,330 --> 01:03:25,670
example to estimate this part.

1198
01:03:25,670 --> 01:03:31,070
And then in this equation,
we'll use one sample, at plus 1

1199
01:03:31,070 --> 01:03:32,670
in order to estimate this.

1200
01:03:32,670 --> 01:03:35,990
There are actually methods that
you can use multiple samples

1201
01:03:35,990 --> 01:03:40,090
to estimate this to get a better
estimate of this expectation.

1202
01:03:40,090 --> 01:03:44,590

1203
01:03:44,590 --> 01:03:48,710
In a batch, you'll be sampling
from your current policy.

1204
01:03:48,710 --> 01:03:51,070
Each data point will
get a different sample,

1205
01:03:51,070 --> 01:03:54,610
so you also get multiple
samples in that way.

1206
01:03:54,610 --> 01:03:56,670
But yeah, even
for next state you

1207
01:03:56,670 --> 01:03:59,750
could also sample
multiple actions here.

1208
01:03:59,750 --> 01:04:03,890
So we mentioned that we need
sufficient action coverage here.

1209
01:04:03,890 --> 01:04:06,390
And the question was, if we use
a KL constraint or something

1210
01:04:06,390 --> 01:04:09,530
similar to encourage it to stay
close to the previous policy,

1211
01:04:09,530 --> 01:04:11,830
would that be helpful here?

1212
01:04:11,830 --> 01:04:16,150
And I think that one of the
algorithms that we'll see

1213
01:04:16,150 --> 01:04:20,850
doesn't do that, but
it's a good idea.

1214
01:04:20,850 --> 01:04:24,920
And in general, we'll see that
those KL constraints come up

1215
01:04:24,920 --> 01:04:26,670
quite frequently in
reinforcement learning

1216
01:04:26,670 --> 01:04:29,380
because we'll often be assuming
that we're not deviating too

1217
01:04:29,380 --> 01:04:34,240
far from our previous policy.

1218
01:04:34,240 --> 01:04:37,993
And yeah, it's possible
for the algorithm

1219
01:04:37,993 --> 01:04:39,660
that we'll see that
could make it better

1220
01:04:39,660 --> 01:04:41,000
in some circumstances too.

1221
01:04:41,000 --> 01:04:48,113

1222
01:04:48,113 --> 01:04:49,780
Well, so I guess what
we talked about is

1223
01:04:49,780 --> 01:04:53,820
we'll be updating Q hat with our
targets y, where the target is

1224
01:04:53,820 --> 01:04:58,980
r plus the Q. And
critically, the state Si

1225
01:04:58,980 --> 01:05:00,940
will be sampled from
the replay buffer.

1226
01:05:00,940 --> 01:05:05,000
But the next action will not
be from the replay buffer.

1227
01:05:05,000 --> 01:05:07,380
It will be sampled from
our policy conditioned

1228
01:05:07,380 --> 01:05:09,230
on that next state
from the buffer.

1229
01:05:09,230 --> 01:05:12,420

1230
01:05:12,420 --> 01:05:17,580
So that will be our revised
step 3 for the algorithm.

1231
01:05:17,580 --> 01:05:19,840
The last thing is what
we want to do here.

1232
01:05:19,840 --> 01:05:24,060
So instead of estimating
the value function,

1233
01:05:24,060 --> 01:05:27,960
we estimate the Q function.

1234
01:05:27,960 --> 01:05:35,040
There's a couple of things that
we'll do here for this update.

1235
01:05:35,040 --> 01:05:37,800
The first is that it'll
be convenient to use

1236
01:05:37,800 --> 01:05:40,480
Q instead of a.

1237
01:05:40,480 --> 01:05:42,960
And this means that we're
not going to use a baseline.

1238
01:05:42,960 --> 01:05:44,460
This is going to
be higher variance,

1239
01:05:44,460 --> 01:05:46,085
but that's going to
be OK because we're

1240
01:05:46,085 --> 01:05:47,640
going to be using
a lot more data,

1241
01:05:47,640 --> 01:05:49,680
all the data from our
buffer, not just the data

1242
01:05:49,680 --> 01:05:52,360
from our current policy.

1243
01:05:52,360 --> 01:05:55,760
And then the second is that
the current policy's actions

1244
01:05:55,760 --> 01:05:58,060
are likely better than
the past policy's actions.

1245
01:05:58,060 --> 01:06:01,560
And so for this
update right here,

1246
01:06:01,560 --> 01:06:04,960
we'll be sampling an a
from our current policy

1247
01:06:04,960 --> 01:06:07,960
instead from the action
taken in the buffer.

1248
01:06:07,960 --> 01:06:11,960
And then this will be the
gradient update on our policy

1249
01:06:11,960 --> 01:06:17,560
where we'll be looking at log
pi of a given s times Q of s, a.

1250
01:06:17,560 --> 01:06:19,618
And these a's will be
sampled from our policy.

1251
01:06:19,618 --> 01:06:22,160
So essentially using the same
trick as what we did with the Q

1252
01:06:22,160 --> 01:06:24,240
function, except now
for the current state

1253
01:06:24,240 --> 01:06:27,900
instead of the next state.

1254
01:06:27,900 --> 01:06:30,500
So here's our full algorithm.

1255
01:06:30,500 --> 01:06:32,540
There's one remaining
problem, which

1256
01:06:32,540 --> 01:06:35,820
is that the states
in our buffer didn't

1257
01:06:35,820 --> 01:06:38,220
come from our current policy.

1258
01:06:38,220 --> 01:06:40,660
There isn't really anything
that we can do here.

1259
01:06:40,660 --> 01:06:42,760
But it's actually not
necessarily a bad thing.

1260
01:06:42,760 --> 01:06:45,302
It means that we're going to be
getting a policy on a broader

1261
01:06:45,302 --> 01:06:48,780
state distribution rather than
just on the state distribution

1262
01:06:48,780 --> 01:06:49,970
that our policy would visit.

1263
01:06:49,970 --> 01:06:52,940

1264
01:06:52,940 --> 01:06:58,800
So this is essentially the
soft actor-critic algorithm.

1265
01:06:58,800 --> 01:07:01,860
There are other ways to estimate
this gradient that we don't have

1266
01:07:01,860 --> 01:07:04,020
time to get into today,
but we might go over

1267
01:07:04,020 --> 01:07:05,500
in future lectures.

1268
01:07:05,500 --> 01:07:08,540
There are also some
fancier ways to fit this Q

1269
01:07:08,540 --> 01:07:11,620
function that we'll see
in the next lecture.

1270
01:07:11,620 --> 01:07:15,420
But the algorithm here that
this is very, very close to

1271
01:07:15,420 --> 01:07:18,820
is called soft
actor-critic or SAC.

1272
01:07:18,820 --> 01:07:22,940
And if we compare SAC and PPO,
the more off-policy version

1273
01:07:22,940 --> 01:07:25,540
that uses a replay buffer
is shown in orange,

1274
01:07:25,540 --> 01:07:27,978
and the less off-policy
is shown in brown.

1275
01:07:27,978 --> 01:07:30,520
And so we see that we're able
to get much more data efficient

1276
01:07:30,520 --> 01:07:34,560
learning with something
like SAC than with something

1277
01:07:34,560 --> 01:07:36,890
that's less off-policy.

1278
01:07:36,890 --> 01:07:38,640
So you can be far, far
more data efficient

1279
01:07:38,640 --> 01:07:40,408
when you use this replay buffer.

1280
01:07:40,408 --> 01:07:42,200
The downside is that
it can be a lot harder

1281
01:07:42,200 --> 01:07:43,908
to tune hyperparameters,
and generally it

1282
01:07:43,908 --> 01:07:46,560
can be a bit less stable
when you're using these Q

1283
01:07:46,560 --> 01:07:49,660
functions than compared to PPO.

1284
01:07:49,660 --> 01:07:50,160
Cool.

1285
01:07:50,160 --> 01:07:52,500
And then lastly, when
you're fully off-policy,

1286
01:07:52,500 --> 01:07:54,000
you can actually
be efficient enough

1287
01:07:54,000 --> 01:07:56,780
to run reinforcement
learning on real robots.

1288
01:07:56,780 --> 01:08:00,680
So in this example,
in about two hours,

1289
01:08:00,680 --> 01:08:05,400
the policy learns how to walk
forward just with the algorithm

1290
01:08:05,400 --> 01:08:07,160
that we talked about.

1291
01:08:07,160 --> 01:08:09,760
You can also learn some
cool manipulation tasks

1292
01:08:09,760 --> 01:08:11,720
with reinforcement learning.

1293
01:08:11,720 --> 01:08:13,220
This was seeded
with demonstrations.

1294
01:08:13,220 --> 01:08:14,685
This is a very precise task.

1295
01:08:14,685 --> 01:08:16,560
And you can see that
actually, if you compare

1296
01:08:16,560 --> 01:08:18,580
the performance
of this RL method,

1297
01:08:18,580 --> 01:08:21,707
it actually gets 100% on all
the tasks that they tested.

1298
01:08:21,707 --> 01:08:23,290
Whereas if you use
imitation learning,

1299
01:08:23,290 --> 01:08:25,590
you'll get a much
lower success rate.

1300
01:08:25,590 --> 01:08:28,830
And reinforcement learning
also leads to a faster policy

1301
01:08:28,830 --> 01:08:33,270
than with imitation learning.

1302
01:08:33,270 --> 01:08:35,270
And then PPO is a
common algorithm

1303
01:08:35,270 --> 01:08:38,178
that's used if you want really
stable learning but less data

1304
01:08:38,178 --> 01:08:38,970
efficient learning.

1305
01:08:38,970 --> 01:08:41,093
And so if you're in
simulation, for example,

1306
01:08:41,093 --> 01:08:42,010
data is not a problem.

1307
01:08:42,010 --> 01:08:44,177
You can collect tons and
tons of data in simulation.

1308
01:08:44,177 --> 01:08:46,090
And you can learn a
policy in simulation.

1309
01:08:46,090 --> 01:08:49,250
And then if you carefully
tune your simulator,

1310
01:08:49,250 --> 01:08:52,069
that policy might be able to
transfer to the real world.

1311
01:08:52,069 --> 01:08:54,630
And then there was
also an example of--

1312
01:08:54,630 --> 01:08:57,229
in general, for manipulation,
send to real transfer

1313
01:08:57,229 --> 01:08:58,170
is a lot harder.

1314
01:08:58,170 --> 01:09:00,390
We're learning how
to do a Rubik's cube

1315
01:09:00,390 --> 01:09:02,050
that was trained in simulation.

1316
01:09:02,050 --> 01:09:05,950
And after about five
minutes of this rollout,

1317
01:09:05,950 --> 01:09:09,790
you get a Rubik's cube
that has been completed.

1318
01:09:09,790 --> 01:09:12,189
And that was with PPO.

1319
01:09:12,189 --> 01:09:14,250
PPO is also a common
choice for language models.

1320
01:09:14,250 --> 01:09:17,020
We'll start to discuss
them next week.

1321
01:09:17,020 --> 01:09:21,000

